window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-06-1", "source": "2022年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “Nowadays more and more people choose to live an environmentally friendly lifestyle.” You can make comments, cite examples, or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Nowadays more and more people choose to live an environmentally friendly lifestyle. This shift is no longer a\nslogan but a set of daily habits that reshape how we eat, travel and consume.\nSeveral concrete practices explain this trend. To begin with, many commuters now favour buses, subways or\nshared bikes over private cars, which cuts carbon emission and eases traffic. What is more, sorting rubbish into\nrecyclable and kitchen waste has become routine in my own neighbourhood, so that plastic and paper can be\nrecycled rather than buried. Take myself as an example: since I started carrying a cloth bag and a reusable\nbottle, I have thrown away far less single-use plastic every week. Small as these acts seem, they add up to a\nsustainable way of living.\nAs a result, an environmentally friendly lifestyle benefits both individuals and the planet. It saves resources,\nkeeps the air cleaner and reminds us that a green future depends on the choices we make today. I firmly believe\nthat when everyone takes one small step, the earth will thank us tomorrow."}, "translation": {"prompt": "卢沟桥位于天安门广场西南15公里处，横跨永定河，是北京现存最古老的多拱石桥。卢沟桥最初建成于1192年，1698年重建，由281根柱子支撑。每根柱子上都有一头石狮。这些石狮的头、背、腹部或爪子上都藏着更多的狮子。这些石狮生动逼真、千姿百态，是卢沟桥石刻艺术的精品。桥上的石狮不计其数，因而北京地区流传着\"卢沟桥上的石狮子——数不清\"的说法。 卢沟桥不仅以其美学特征闻名于世，还被公认为石桥建筑史上的一座丰碑。", "sample": "Located 15 kilometres southwest of Tian'anmen Square and spanning the Yongding River, Lugou Bridge is the oldest existing multi-arch stone bridge in Beijing. It was first built in 1192 and rebuilt in 1698, and is supported by 281 pillars. On each pillar stands a stone lion, and on the heads, backs, bellies or paws of these stone lions are hidden even more lions. Vivid and lifelike and varying in posture, these stone lions are regarded as the finest works of stone carving on Lugou Bridge. The stone lions on the bridge are too numerous to count, so there is a popular saying in the Beijing area: \"The stone lions on Lugou Bridge are simply countless.\" Lugou Bridge is not only world-famous for its aesthetic features but also widely recognized as a monument in the history of stone bridge construction."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2022-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The city of Bath was founded by the Romans almost two thousand years ago. It has been famous for its  __26__  pleasing architecture and healing thermal springs ever since.\nThere are three hot springs in Bath; one is the King's Spring, upon which the Roman Baths and a temple were  __27__  . The other two are the Cross Spring and the Hetling Spring, close to each other in Hot Bath Street. Although Bath is  __28__  known as a Roman and Georgian city, many people came in the intervening centuries to make use of the  __29__  waters.\nWhile the Georgians made 'taking the waters' or bathing particularly fashionable, it was  __30__  generations who paved the way, creating greater interest in Bath and its springs. Charles II, desperate for an heir and unable to produce a  __31__  son, came to Bath to take the waters in the hope that their magical powers would do something to  __32__  the situation. Craving for a male heir, James and Mary both came to Bath and soon after produced a son, which bred many conspiracy theories about who was the real father of their  __33__  . Regardless, the 'miracle' created something of a boom in tourism for Bath and once Queen Anne had paid a visit in 1702, sealing it as the place to be, the whole nation  __34__  to the city.\nAfterwards, the spas(矿泉疗养浴场) in Bath continued to go in and out of fashion for more than 150 years until they closed completely. The new Bath Spa, which opened in 2006,  __35__  modern architecture with the ancient spring, now the New Royal Bath.", "matchIntro": "The Doctor Will Skype You Now", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Fazila is a young woman that has been dealing with eczema(湿疹), a common skin condition, for the past five years, but never got it treated. The nearest hospital is an hour away, by boat and bus, and her skin condition didn't seem serious enough to make the trek, so she ignored it—until a new technology brought the doctor to her. Fazila lives on one of the remote river islands in northern Bangladesh. These islands are low-lying, temporary sand islands that are continuously formed and destroyed through sand buildup and erosion. They are home to over six million people, who face repeated displacement from flooding and erosion—which may be getting worse because of climate change—and a range of health risks, including poor nutrition, malaria(疟疾) and other water-borne diseases."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The most dangerous thing for these remote island dwellers is land erosion. The second is lack of access to medical supplies and doctors. There are no doctors within miles, and while child mortality and maternal death have gone down in the rest of the country, this is not the case for the islands. The medical situation is so bad that it really takes away from the quality of their life. Yet for many island inhabitants—some of Bangladesh's poorest—paying for health care is a costly ordeal. Victims of erosion lose their houses, agricultural land and jobs as farmers, fishermen and day laborers. Though government hospitals are free, many people hesitate to go, citing long commutes, endless lines and questionable diagnoses. For convenience's sake, one-third of rural households visit unqualified village doctors, who rely on unscientific methods of treatment, according to a 2016 study in the peer-reviewed journal Global Health Action."}, {"label": "C", "text": "On the islands, there's even a colloquial(口头的) expression for the idea of making medical care your lowest priority: It's known as \"rog pushai rakha\" in Bengali, which roughly translates to \"stockpiling their diseases\"—waiting to seek medical attention until a condition becomes extremely serious. Now, a new virtual medical service called Teledaktar (TD) is trying to make health care more easily accessible. Every week, TD's medical operators travel to the islands by boat, carrying a laptop, a portable printer for prescriptions and tools to run basic medical screenings such as blood pressure, blood sugar, body temperature and weight. They choose an area of the island with the best Internet reception and set up a makeshift(临时凑合的) medical center which consists of plastic stools and small tables borrowed from the locals' homes, a tent in case of rain and a sheet that is strung up to give the patients privacy during their session."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Launched in October 2018, TD has eight centers in towns and villages across rural Bangladesh and on three islands. It is funded by a nonprofit organization founded by Bangladeshi entrepreneurs, finance and technology professionals. Inside the center, the laptop screen lights up to reveal Dr. Tina Mustahid, TD's head physician, live-streamed(网络直播) from the capital city of Dhaka for free remote medical consultations. Affectionately called Doctor Apa—\"older sister\" in Bengali—by her patients, she is one of three volunteer doctors at TD."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"I diagnose them through conversation,\" says Dr. Mustahid. \"Sometimes it's really obvious things that local doctors don't have the patience to talk through with their patients. For example, a common complaint mothers come in with is that their children refuse to eat their meals. The mothers are concerned they are dealing with indigestion, but it's because they are feeding the children packaged chips which are cheap and convenient. I tell them it is ruining their appetite and ask them to cut back on unhealthy snacks.\" Dr. Mustahid says building awareness about health and nutrition is important for island patients who are cut off from mainland resources."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Even off the islands, Bangladesh faces a critical deficit of health services. The country has half the doctors-per-person ratio recommended by the World Health Organization: roughly one doctor per 2,000 people, instead of one doctor per 1,000 people. And of those physicians, many are concentrated in cities: 70% of the country's population live in rural areas, yet less than 20% of health workers practice there. Over 70% of TD's 3,000 patients are female, in part because many are not comfortable speaking with local doctors who tend to be male. The rural women are mostly not literate or confident enough to travel on their own to the nearest town to visit medical facilities. Many have spent their entire lives rebuilding their homes when the islands flood. Early marriage and young motherhood, which are prevalent in these parts of Bangladesh, also contribute to the early onset of health problems."}, {"label": "G", "text": "For most TD patients on the islands, Dr. Mustahid is the first big-city doctor that they've ever consulted. TD doctors are not meant to treat serious illnesses or conditions that require a doctor to be physically present, such as pregnancy. But they can write prescriptions, diagnose common ailments—including digestive issues, joint pain, skin diseases, fever and the common cold—and refer patients to doctors at local hospitals. The visit is also an opportunity for the patients, especially women, to air their concerns about aging, motherhood and reproductive health according to Dr. Mustahid. The doctors also offer health, dietary and lifestyle advice where necessary, including insight on everything from recognizing postnatal(产后的) depression to daily exercise. Dr. Mustahid regularly recommends her patients to take a daily thirty-minute morning walk before the sun gets too intense."}, {"label": "H", "text": "After a few sessions about general health issues Fazila finally opened up about something else that was bothering her: her persistent skin condition. It can get expensive to travel to the doctor, so usually the women living on the islands describe their illness to their husbands. The husbands then go to the pharmacy, try to describe the issue and return home with some random medicines. Nothing worked for Fazila until she started seeing Dr. Apa."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Other nonprofits are also starting to provide health services on the islands. A local non-governmental organization called Friendship operates floating boat hospitals that provide health services to islands all over Bangladesh, docking at each for two months at a time. Friendship also runs satellite clinics in which one doctor and one clinic aide who are residents of the community disperse health and hygiene information."}, {"label": "J", "text": "TD still has a few major challenges. Many residents complain the medicines they are prescribed are sometimes unaffordable, but the government isn't doing enough for them. Patients often ask why the medicine isn't free along with the consultation from the doctors. The organizations are linked to local pharmacies and offer discounts to the patients and make sure to prescribe the most cost-effective brands, but still many residents can't afford even that."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Nevertheless, TD's remote consultations seem to be popular: Of 3,000 patients, at least 200 have returned for follow-ups, according to TD. The reason, explains one resident, might be the simple gesture of treating the island inhabitants with respect. \"Dr. Apa is patient,\" he says, \"At government hospitals, the doctors treat us very badly, but here they listen to us, I can repeat myself many times and no one gets annoyed.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Selective colleges and universities in the U.S. are under fire for being too elite and too expensive, and for not training graduates for the world of work. Such charges ignore the fact that these institutions continue to prepare students for success in their work, for thoughtful engagement in civic life, for lifelong learning, and for understanding the world and those with whom they live.\nThese colleges and universities must be doing something right. Applications are at record highs, and their financial aid programs make them more accessible than ever. This model of education has long played a central role in creating opportunity, driving economic growth, and spurring innovation.\nYet, there is growing skepticism about the value of this model. The recent tax reform bill is a wake-up call that our strongest colleges and universities are under assault by some in government. The initial proposals would have made education unaffordable for many by taxing tuition waivers for graduate students and ending deductions for student loan interest. Thankfully, these provisions were ultimately stripped from the bill, but lawmakers let stand a new tax on the investment income of some colleges and universities.\nWhile these attacks are motivated by misguided ideas, we need to do a better job of explaining why these claims are false and why what we do is valuable. We cannot take for granted that any of this is obvious.\nIt is often said that elite colleges and universities do not train students, particularly those who study the liberal arts, for the workforce. But this can be refuted by scholarly research. The data are clear: a liberal arts education is great career preparation, both for excellent lifetime earnings and for satisfaction with the work. This education develops the skills of critical thinking, rigorous analysis of data and facts, communication with the written and spoken word, understanding of cultural differences and issues, and the ability to keep learning. In fact, liberal arts graduates do extremely well in every imaginable field.\nAccess to an education at selective colleges and universities is now more available than ever to low- and middle-income families. We have built endowments from donations by alumni(校友) and parents who understand and appreciate our mission to provide access and opportunity, and a significant portion of the returns from these endowments is used to fund financial aid.\nIronically, the new tax on endowments drains financial aid funds from the very schools most able to offer opportunity to those who have earned a spot but cannot otherwise afford this education. Beyond the virtue of access to those who have earned a place at these schools, the diversity of economic backgrounds enhances the education and experience of all of our students.", "47": "Selective colleges and universities in the U.S. are under fire for being too elite and too expensive, and for not training graduates for the world of work. Such charges ignore the fact that these institutions continue to prepare students for success in their work, for thoughtful engagement in civic life, for lifelong learning, and for understanding the world and those with whom they live.\nThese colleges and universities must be doing something right. Applications are at record highs, and their financial aid programs make them more accessible than ever. This model of education has long played a central role in creating opportunity, driving economic growth, and spurring innovation.\nYet, there is growing skepticism about the value of this model. The recent tax reform bill is a wake-up call that our strongest colleges and universities are under assault by some in government. The initial proposals would have made education unaffordable for many by taxing tuition waivers for graduate students and ending deductions for student loan interest. Thankfully, these provisions were ultimately stripped from the bill, but lawmakers let stand a new tax on the investment income of some colleges and universities.\nWhile these attacks are motivated by misguided ideas, we need to do a better job of explaining why these claims are false and why what we do is valuable. We cannot take for granted that any of this is obvious.\nIt is often said that elite colleges and universities do not train students, particularly those who study the liberal arts, for the workforce. But this can be refuted by scholarly research. The data are clear: a liberal arts education is great career preparation, both for excellent lifetime earnings and for satisfaction with the work. This education develops the skills of critical thinking, rigorous analysis of data and facts, communication with the written and spoken word, understanding of cultural differences and issues, and the ability to keep learning. In fact, liberal arts graduates do extremely well in every imaginable field.\nAccess to an education at selective colleges and universities is now more available than ever to low- and middle-income families. We have built endowments from donations by alumni(校友) and parents who understand and appreciate our mission to provide access and opportunity, and a significant portion of the returns from these endowments is used to fund financial aid.\nIronically, the new tax on endowments drains financial aid funds from the very schools most able to offer opportunity to those who have earned a spot but cannot otherwise afford this education. Beyond the virtue of access to those who have earned a place at these schools, the diversity of economic backgrounds enhances the education and experience of all of our students.", "48": "Selective colleges and universities in the U.S. are under fire for being too elite and too expensive, and for not training graduates for the world of work. Such charges ignore the fact that these institutions continue to prepare students for success in their work, for thoughtful engagement in civic life, for lifelong learning, and for understanding the world and those with whom they live.\nThese colleges and universities must be doing something right. Applications are at record highs, and their financial aid programs make them more accessible than ever. This model of education has long played a central role in creating opportunity, driving economic growth, and spurring innovation.\nYet, there is growing skepticism about the value of this model. The recent tax reform bill is a wake-up call that our strongest colleges and universities are under assault by some in government. The initial proposals would have made education unaffordable for many by taxing tuition waivers for graduate students and ending deductions for student loan interest. Thankfully, these provisions were ultimately stripped from the bill, but lawmakers let stand a new tax on the investment income of some colleges and universities.\nWhile these attacks are motivated by misguided ideas, we need to do a better job of explaining why these claims are false and why what we do is valuable. We cannot take for granted that any of this is obvious.\nIt is often said that elite colleges and universities do not train students, particularly those who study the liberal arts, for the workforce. But this can be refuted by scholarly research. The data are clear: a liberal arts education is great career preparation, both for excellent lifetime earnings and for satisfaction with the work. This education develops the skills of critical thinking, rigorous analysis of data and facts, communication with the written and spoken word, understanding of cultural differences and issues, and the ability to keep learning. In fact, liberal arts graduates do extremely well in every imaginable field.\nAccess to an education at selective colleges and universities is now more available than ever to low- and middle-income families. We have built endowments from donations by alumni(校友) and parents who understand and appreciate our mission to provide access and opportunity, and a significant portion of the returns from these endowments is used to fund financial aid.\nIronically, the new tax on endowments drains financial aid funds from the very schools most able to offer opportunity to those who have earned a spot but cannot otherwise afford this education. Beyond the virtue of access to those who have earned a place at these schools, the diversity of economic backgrounds enhances the education and experience of all of our students.", "49": "Selective colleges and universities in the U.S. are under fire for being too elite and too expensive, and for not training graduates for the world of work. Such charges ignore the fact that these institutions continue to prepare students for success in their work, for thoughtful engagement in civic life, for lifelong learning, and for understanding the world and those with whom they live.\nThese colleges and universities must be doing something right. Applications are at record highs, and their financial aid programs make them more accessible than ever. This model of education has long played a central role in creating opportunity, driving economic growth, and spurring innovation.\nYet, there is growing skepticism about the value of this model. The recent tax reform bill is a wake-up call that our strongest colleges and universities are under assault by some in government. The initial proposals would have made education unaffordable for many by taxing tuition waivers for graduate students and ending deductions for student loan interest. Thankfully, these provisions were ultimately stripped from the bill, but lawmakers let stand a new tax on the investment income of some colleges and universities.\nWhile these attacks are motivated by misguided ideas, we need to do a better job of explaining why these claims are false and why what we do is valuable. We cannot take for granted that any of this is obvious.\nIt is often said that elite colleges and universities do not train students, particularly those who study the liberal arts, for the workforce. But this can be refuted by scholarly research. The data are clear: a liberal arts education is great career preparation, both for excellent lifetime earnings and for satisfaction with the work. This education develops the skills of critical thinking, rigorous analysis of data and facts, communication with the written and spoken word, understanding of cultural differences and issues, and the ability to keep learning. In fact, liberal arts graduates do extremely well in every imaginable field.\nAccess to an education at selective colleges and universities is now more available than ever to low- and middle-income families. We have built endowments from donations by alumni(校友) and parents who understand and appreciate our mission to provide access and opportunity, and a significant portion of the returns from these endowments is used to fund financial aid.\nIronically, the new tax on endowments drains financial aid funds from the very schools most able to offer opportunity to those who have earned a spot but cannot otherwise afford this education. Beyond the virtue of access to those who have earned a place at these schools, the diversity of economic backgrounds enhances the education and experience of all of our students.", "50": "Selective colleges and universities in the U.S. are under fire for being too elite and too expensive, and for not training graduates for the world of work. Such charges ignore the fact that these institutions continue to prepare students for success in their work, for thoughtful engagement in civic life, for lifelong learning, and for understanding the world and those with whom they live.\nThese colleges and universities must be doing something right. Applications are at record highs, and their financial aid programs make them more accessible than ever. This model of education has long played a central role in creating opportunity, driving economic growth, and spurring innovation.\nYet, there is growing skepticism about the value of this model. The recent tax reform bill is a wake-up call that our strongest colleges and universities are under assault by some in government. The initial proposals would have made education unaffordable for many by taxing tuition waivers for graduate students and ending deductions for student loan interest. Thankfully, these provisions were ultimately stripped from the bill, but lawmakers let stand a new tax on the investment income of some colleges and universities.\nWhile these attacks are motivated by misguided ideas, we need to do a better job of explaining why these claims are false and why what we do is valuable. We cannot take for granted that any of this is obvious.\nIt is often said that elite colleges and universities do not train students, particularly those who study the liberal arts, for the workforce. But this can be refuted by scholarly research. The data are clear: a liberal arts education is great career preparation, both for excellent lifetime earnings and for satisfaction with the work. This education develops the skills of critical thinking, rigorous analysis of data and facts, communication with the written and spoken word, understanding of cultural differences and issues, and the ability to keep learning. In fact, liberal arts graduates do extremely well in every imaginable field.\nAccess to an education at selective colleges and universities is now more available than ever to low- and middle-income families. We have built endowments from donations by alumni(校友) and parents who understand and appreciate our mission to provide access and opportunity, and a significant portion of the returns from these endowments is used to fund financial aid.\nIronically, the new tax on endowments drains financial aid funds from the very schools most able to offer opportunity to those who have earned a spot but cannot otherwise afford this education. Beyond the virtue of access to those who have earned a place at these schools, the diversity of economic backgrounds enhances the education and experience of all of our students.", "51": "When a group of Australians was asked why they believed climate change was not happening, about 36% said it was \"common sense\", according to a report published last year by the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organization. This was the most popular reason for their opinion, with only 11% saying their belief that climate change was not happening was based on scientific research.\nBut what do we mean by an appeal to common sense? Presumably it's an appeal to rationality of some sort that forms the basis of more complex reasoning. The appeal to common sense, however, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking that just feels right, but what feels right to one person may not feel right to another. Whether it feels right is usually a reflection of the world view and ideologies we have internalised, and that frames how we interact with new ideas. When new ideas are in accord with what we already believe, they are more readily accepted. When they are not, they, and the arguments that lead to them, are more readily rejected.\nWe often mistake this automatic compatibility testing of new ideas with existing beliefs as an application of common sense, but, in reality, it is more about judging than thinking. As Nobelist Daniel Kahneman notes in Thinking, Fast and Slow, when we arrive at conclusions in this way, the outcomes also feel true, regardless of whether they are. We are not psychologically well equipped to judge our own thinking.\nWe are also highly susceptible to a range of cognitive biases such as giving preference to the first things that come to mind when making decisions or giving weight to evidence.\nOne way we can check our internal biases and inconsistencies is through the social verification of knowledge, in which we test our ideas in a rigorous and systematic way to see if they make sense not just to us, but to other people. The outstanding example of this socially shared cognition is science.\nThat does not mean that individuals are not capable of excellent thinking, nor does it mean no individual is rational. But the extent to which individuals can do this on their own is a function of how well integrated they are with communities of systematic inquiry in the first place. You can't learn to think well by yourself.\nIn matters of science at least, those who value their common sense over methodological, collaborative investigation imagine themselves to be more free in their thinking, unbound by involvement with the group, but in reality they are tightly bound by their capabilities and perspectives. We are smarter together than we are individually, and perhaps that's just common sense.", "52": "When a group of Australians was asked why they believed climate change was not happening, about 36% said it was \"common sense\", according to a report published last year by the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organization. This was the most popular reason for their opinion, with only 11% saying their belief that climate change was not happening was based on scientific research.\nBut what do we mean by an appeal to common sense? Presumably it's an appeal to rationality of some sort that forms the basis of more complex reasoning. The appeal to common sense, however, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking that just feels right, but what feels right to one person may not feel right to another. Whether it feels right is usually a reflection of the world view and ideologies we have internalised, and that frames how we interact with new ideas. When new ideas are in accord with what we already believe, they are more readily accepted. When they are not, they, and the arguments that lead to them, are more readily rejected.\nWe often mistake this automatic compatibility testing of new ideas with existing beliefs as an application of common sense, but, in reality, it is more about judging than thinking. As Nobelist Daniel Kahneman notes in Thinking, Fast and Slow, when we arrive at conclusions in this way, the outcomes also feel true, regardless of whether they are. We are not psychologically well equipped to judge our own thinking.\nWe are also highly susceptible to a range of cognitive biases such as giving preference to the first things that come to mind when making decisions or giving weight to evidence.\nOne way we can check our internal biases and inconsistencies is through the social verification of knowledge, in which we test our ideas in a rigorous and systematic way to see if they make sense not just to us, but to other people. The outstanding example of this socially shared cognition is science.\nThat does not mean that individuals are not capable of excellent thinking, nor does it mean no individual is rational. But the extent to which individuals can do this on their own is a function of how well integrated they are with communities of systematic inquiry in the first place. You can't learn to think well by yourself.\nIn matters of science at least, those who value their common sense over methodological, collaborative investigation imagine themselves to be more free in their thinking, unbound by involvement with the group, but in reality they are tightly bound by their capabilities and perspectives. We are smarter together than we are individually, and perhaps that's just common sense.", "53": "When a group of Australians was asked why they believed climate change was not happening, about 36% said it was \"common sense\", according to a report published last year by the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organization. This was the most popular reason for their opinion, with only 11% saying their belief that climate change was not happening was based on scientific research.\nBut what do we mean by an appeal to common sense? Presumably it's an appeal to rationality of some sort that forms the basis of more complex reasoning. The appeal to common sense, however, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking that just feels right, but what feels right to one person may not feel right to another. Whether it feels right is usually a reflection of the world view and ideologies we have internalised, and that frames how we interact with new ideas. When new ideas are in accord with what we already believe, they are more readily accepted. When they are not, they, and the arguments that lead to them, are more readily rejected.\nWe often mistake this automatic compatibility testing of new ideas with existing beliefs as an application of common sense, but, in reality, it is more about judging than thinking. As Nobelist Daniel Kahneman notes in Thinking, Fast and Slow, when we arrive at conclusions in this way, the outcomes also feel true, regardless of whether they are. We are not psychologically well equipped to judge our own thinking.\nWe are also highly susceptible to a range of cognitive biases such as giving preference to the first things that come to mind when making decisions or giving weight to evidence.\nOne way we can check our internal biases and inconsistencies is through the social verification of knowledge, in which we test our ideas in a rigorous and systematic way to see if they make sense not just to us, but to other people. The outstanding example of this socially shared cognition is science.\nThat does not mean that individuals are not capable of excellent thinking, nor does it mean no individual is rational. But the extent to which individuals can do this on their own is a function of how well integrated they are with communities of systematic inquiry in the first place. You can't learn to think well by yourself.\nIn matters of science at least, those who value their common sense over methodological, collaborative investigation imagine themselves to be more free in their thinking, unbound by involvement with the group, but in reality they are tightly bound by their capabilities and perspectives. We are smarter together than we are individually, and perhaps that's just common sense.", "54": "When a group of Australians was asked why they believed climate change was not happening, about 36% said it was \"common sense\", according to a report published last year by the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organization. This was the most popular reason for their opinion, with only 11% saying their belief that climate change was not happening was based on scientific research.\nBut what do we mean by an appeal to common sense? Presumably it's an appeal to rationality of some sort that forms the basis of more complex reasoning. The appeal to common sense, however, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking that just feels right, but what feels right to one person may not feel right to another. Whether it feels right is usually a reflection of the world view and ideologies we have internalised, and that frames how we interact with new ideas. When new ideas are in accord with what we already believe, they are more readily accepted. When they are not, they, and the arguments that lead to them, are more readily rejected.\nWe often mistake this automatic compatibility testing of new ideas with existing beliefs as an application of common sense, but, in reality, it is more about judging than thinking. As Nobelist Daniel Kahneman notes in Thinking, Fast and Slow, when we arrive at conclusions in this way, the outcomes also feel true, regardless of whether they are. We are not psychologically well equipped to judge our own thinking.\nWe are also highly susceptible to a range of cognitive biases such as giving preference to the first things that come to mind when making decisions or giving weight to evidence.\nOne way we can check our internal biases and inconsistencies is through the social verification of knowledge, in which we test our ideas in a rigorous and systematic way to see if they make sense not just to us, but to other people. The outstanding example of this socially shared cognition is science.\nThat does not mean that individuals are not capable of excellent thinking, nor does it mean no individual is rational. But the extent to which individuals can do this on their own is a function of how well integrated they are with communities of systematic inquiry in the first place. You can't learn to think well by yourself.\nIn matters of science at least, those who value their common sense over methodological, collaborative investigation imagine themselves to be more free in their thinking, unbound by involvement with the group, but in reality they are tightly bound by their capabilities and perspectives. We are smarter together than we are individually, and perhaps that's just common sense.", "55": "When a group of Australians was asked why they believed climate change was not happening, about 36% said it was \"common sense\", according to a report published last year by the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organization. This was the most popular reason for their opinion, with only 11% saying their belief that climate change was not happening was based on scientific research.\nBut what do we mean by an appeal to common sense? Presumably it's an appeal to rationality of some sort that forms the basis of more complex reasoning. The appeal to common sense, however, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking that just feels right, but what feels right to one person may not feel right to another. Whether it feels right is usually a reflection of the world view and ideologies we have internalised, and that frames how we interact with new ideas. When new ideas are in accord with what we already believe, they are more readily accepted. When they are not, they, and the arguments that lead to them, are more readily rejected.\nWe often mistake this automatic compatibility testing of new ideas with existing beliefs as an application of common sense, but, in reality, it is more about judging than thinking. As Nobelist Daniel Kahneman notes in Thinking, Fast and Slow, when we arrive at conclusions in this way, the outcomes also feel true, regardless of whether they are. We are not psychologically well equipped to judge our own thinking.\nWe are also highly susceptible to a range of cognitive biases such as giving preference to the first things that come to mind when making decisions or giving weight to evidence.\nOne way we can check our internal biases and inconsistencies is through the social verification of knowledge, in which we test our ideas in a rigorous and systematic way to see if they make sense not just to us, but to other people. The outstanding example of this socially shared cognition is science.\nThat does not mean that individuals are not capable of excellent thinking, nor does it mean no individual is rational. But the extent to which individuals can do this on their own is a function of how well integrated they are with communities of systematic inquiry in the first place. You can't learn to think well by yourself.\nIn matters of science at least, those who value their common sense over methodological, collaborative investigation imagine themselves to be more free in their thinking, unbound by involvement with the group, but in reality they are tightly bound by their capabilities and perspectives. We are smarter together than we are individually, and perhaps that's just common sense."}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about David Jackson from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He is a staff writer.", "B) He is an adventurer.", "C) He is an author of fiction.", "D) He is a father of four kids."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He is a staff writer.\n【定位】Mr. David Jackson, a staff writer at the New Yorker, is known for his non-fiction books\nof adventure.\nDavid Jackson 先生，《纽约客》的专职撰稿人，以其纪实类冒险题材的书而闻名。\n【信号】开场介绍句直接报身份。主持人开场第一句就用同位语把人物头衔报全：a staff writer at\nthe New Yorker。人物介绍题的答案信号就是这种\"姓名 + 同位语头衔\"的引入句，听到立\n刻锁定。\n【替换】a staff writer at the New Yorker ↔ He is a staff writer.\n原句同位语→选项主系表，几乎原词对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文说他 is known for his non-fiction books of adventure——写冒险题材的书，是\"写\n冒险的人\"不是\"亲历冒险的人\"。He is an adventurer. 盗用 adventure 一词，把\"写冒险\n书\"偷换成\"他本人是冒险家\"，听到 adventure 就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：is known for his non-fiction books of adventure\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明确是 non-fiction books（纪实/非虚构），He is an author of fiction. 却说他写\nfiction（虚构小说），正好把 non-fiction 的 non 抹掉、极性反了。听到 fiction 这半个原\n词就选，等于没听清前面的否定前缀。\n借⽤原⽂：non-fiction books of adventure\nD) 未提及\n原文只说他有 an 11- and a 14-year-old（一个 11 岁、一个 14 岁，共两个孩子），He is\na father of four kids. 凭空说\"四个孩子\"。four 这个数字原文从未出现，是偷改数字的陷\n阱。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man think of young teenagers?", "options": ["A) They are interested in fairy tales.", "B) They are curious and autonomous.", "C) They are a headache to their parents.", "D) They are ignorant of politics."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They are curious and autonomous.\n【定位】They are independent, and they have their own curiosities and obsessions.\n他们很独立，有自己的好奇心和着迷的东西。\n【信号】被问者的正面评价应答。女方问他这阶段孩子的情况，男方应答先说 I find these ages to\nbe kind of wonderful，再具体说 They are independent, and they have their own\ncuriosities。对话题答案落在被问者的应答里，wonderful 与 independent、curiosities\n是定调的正面信号。\n【替换】They are independent ↔ They are curious and autonomous.\nindependent=autonomous 同义替换\ntheir own curiosities ↔ curious\n名词 curiosities→形容词 curious 词形转换\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文说能和孩子聊 sophisticated subject matter such as politics，即谈\"成熟话题如政\n治\"，反衬他们不幼稚；They are interested in fairy tales. 童话与原文要表达的\"能聊成熟\n话题\"恰恰相反，且 fairy tales 原文根本没提，是借\"孩子\"语境硬造的话题。\n借⽤原⽂：sophisticated subject matter such as politics\nC) 与原文相反\n男方明明说 I find these ages to be kind of wonderful（觉得这年纪很好），They are a\nheadache to their parents. 却说孩子让父母头疼，与他的正面评价完全相反。这是把褒义\n答案反向设成贬义的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：I find these ages to be kind of wonderful\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文说能和孩子聊 politics，恰恰说明他们\"懂/能聊政治\"；They are ignorant of politics.\n盗用 politics 原词，却说他们\"对政治无知\"，意思正好反过来。听到 politics 就选会直接掉\n坑。\n借⽤原⽂：such as politics", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "How does the man help his kids with their essays?", "options": ["A) He offers them ample editorial guidance.", "B) He recommends model essays to them.", "C) He gives them encouragement.", "D) He teaches them proofreading."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He gives them encouragement.\n【定位】I really just try to be encouraging. I think at this age, editorial guidance is less important\nthan encouragement.\n我真的只是尽量给予鼓励。我觉得在这个年纪，编辑式的指导不如鼓励来得重要。\n【信号】取舍句 less important than 落在后半。男方先表态 I really just try to be\nencouraging，再用取舍句 editorial guidance is less important than\nencouragement。\"A is less important than B\" 的信号是：被贬低的 A（指导）是陪衬，\n强调的 B（鼓励）才是答案。\n【替换】I really just try to be encouraging ↔ He gives them encouragement.\nbe encouraging→give encouragement 形容词转名词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 He offers them ample editorial guidance. 盗用原文 editorial guidance 原词，可\n原文恰恰说它 less important than encouragement——他不强调指导。听到 editorial\nguidance 就选，正好掉进\"被贬低项\"的坑，且 ample（大量）更与他\"只是尽量鼓励\"的克\n制相反。\n借⽤原⽂：editorial guidance is less important than encouragement\nB) 未提及\nHe recommends model essays to them. 借了 essays 这个语境词（女方确实问到\nproofread their essays），但\"推荐范文（model essays）\"这件事男方从没说过，是凭\"帮\n写作\"语境硬造的做法。\nD) 张冠李戴\nproofread 这个词确实出现，但它出自女方提问 Do they ask you to proofread their\nessays?，是\"孩子请他校对\"，并非\"他教孩子校对\"。He teaches them proofreading. 把\n女方问句里的词搬来、又改成\"他教校对\"，既盗原词又错了施动方。\n借⽤原⽂：Do they ask you to proofread their essays?", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say about herself when she was a teenager?", "options": ["A) Her tastes in books changed.", "B) She realized the power of reading.", "C) Her reading opened her eyes to the world.", "D) She began to perceive the world differently."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Her tastes in books changed.\n【定位】I feel that children's tastes in books change as they reach adolescence. I know that mine\ncertainly did when I was a teenager.\n我觉得孩子的读书口味会随着进入青春期而改变。我知道我自己当年读中学时口味确实\n变了。\n【信号】mine + when I was a teenager 锁定她谈自己。女方说 I know that mine certainly did\nwhen I was a teenager，mine 回指 tastes in books，when I was a teenager 正对题干\n限定。第一人称 mine/I 加\"少年时\"，是\"她说自己\"的答案信号。\n【替换】children's tastes in books change ↔ Her tastes in books changed.\nmine did 还原为 her tastes changed\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nShe realized the power of reading. 里的 power 出自男方 they give you the power to\nbegin to perceive the world，是男方谈读书的话，不是女方说自己；且女方从没说她\"意\n识到读书的力量\"。既错主语又属未提及。\n借⽤原⽂：they give you the power to begin to perceive the world\nC) 张冠李戴\nHer reading opened her eyes to the world. 盗用男方 read subject matter that'll open\ntheir eyes to the world 的原词 open eyes to the world，但这是男方对\"孩子该读什么\"的\n看法，主语既不是\"她\"、内容也非她自述。听到 open eyes 就选会被男方的话误导。\n借⽤原⽂：open their eyes to the world\nD) 原词陷阱\nShe began to perceive the world differently. 盗用男方 to perceive the world through\nthe lives of others 的 perceive the world，但这同样是男方对读书意义的论述，非女方谈\n自己。与 C 是一对孪生干扰，靠\"主语是谁\"才能切开。\n借⽤原⽂：perceive the world through the lives of others", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman?", "options": ["A) She is a website designer.", "B) She is a university graduate.", "C) She is a main street store owner.", "D) She is a successful entrepreneur."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is a successful entrepreneur.\n【定位】In this episode of Money Talks, our guest is Molly Sanders, a university student and a\nsuccessful young entrepreneur.\n在本期《财富对话》中，我们的嘉宾是莫莉·桑德斯，一名大学生，也是一位成功的年轻创\n业者。\n【信号】主持人开场介绍嘉宾的同位语。答案信号是开场白里介绍嘉宾身份的同位语 a university\nstudent and a successful young entrepreneur——访谈类听力里，嘉宾身份几乎都在主\n持人开场这句 our guest is… 后面交代。\n【替换】a successful young entrepreneur ↔ She is a successful entrepreneur.\n近乎同词，只省去 young\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说她是 a university student，She is a university graduate. 借此设套；但一来\n题问我们了解到什么，主持人重点捧的是 successful entrepreneur，二来 student（在\n读）不等于 graduate（已毕业），身份还被偷改了。\n借⽤原⽂：a university student\nA) 过度推断\n后文她说 I sell specialty clothes through a website，但用网站卖衣服不等于她是做网站\n的设计师，She is a website designer. 是从 website 一词过度联想出来的。\n借⽤原⽂：I sell specialty clothes through a website\nC) 与原文相反\n她明确说将来 I'll keep things online to keep costs down，根本不开实体店，She is a\nmain street store owner. 与原文相反；main street 还是她顾客买不到衣服的地方，并非\n她开店之处。\n借⽤原⽂：I'll keep things online to keep costs down", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the woman say about the clothes she made at first?", "options": ["A) They were repeatedly rejected by shops.", "B) They were popular with her classmates.", "C) They showed her natural talent.", "D) They were mostly failures."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They were mostly failures.\n【定位】No one taught me to make clothes and most of the things I made at first were disasters.\n没人教过我做衣服，而且我最初做的大多数东西都是惨不忍睹的失败品。\n【信号】被问者直接应答里的自我评价词。主持人问她是否有天赋，她应答中的 most of the things\nI made at first were disasters 是直接评价句，disasters 这个强贬义词就是答案信号。\n【替换】were disasters ↔ They were mostly failures.\ndisasters 约等于 failures 的同义替换\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n主持人问 Or was it a natural ability?，They showed her natural talent. 盗用了 natural\n一词；但她明确回答 I'd have to say no to both（天赋、培训都没有），所以显出天赋恰\n与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Or was it a natural ability?\nA) 未提及\nThey were repeatedly rejected by shops. 听着像创业故事，但录音从没说她最初的衣服\n被商店反复拒收；她说的是顾客在 main street shops 买不到合身的，张冠李戴了。\nB) 张冠李戴\n同学称赞她衣服确有其事——some classmates complimented my clothes，They were\npopular with her classmates. 借此设套；但那是她上大学后的事，不是最初（14岁）做\n的，时间错位。\n借⽤原⽂：some classmates complimented my clothes", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why did the woman persevere in making clothes for herself?", "options": ["A) She had a strong interest in doing it.", "B) She did not like ready-made clothes.", "C) She could not find clothes of her size.", "D) She found clothes in shops unaffordable."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She could not find clothes of her size.\n【定位】I kept on out of necessity. As you can see, I'm very tall and I couldn't find clothes that fit me\nin ordinary shops.\n我是出于无奈才坚持下来的。如你所见，我个子很高，在普通商店里找不到合身的衣服。\n【信号】原因短语 out of necessity 引出理由。答案信号是因果短语 out of necessity（出于必\n需），其后紧跟的 I'm very tall and I couldn't find clothes that fit me 就是真正原因。\n【替换】I couldn't find clothes that fit me in ordinary shops ↔ She could not find clothes of her size.\nfit me 约等于 of her size 的同义改写\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n她确说过 I've always loved clothes，She had a strong interest in doing it. 借喜爱设\n套；但那解释的是她为何开始做衣服，题问的是为何坚持，她坚持的理由是无奈，不是兴\n趣。\n借⽤原⽂：I've always loved clothes\nD) 未提及\nShe found clothes in shops unaffordable. 与C都涉及商店的衣服，迷惑性强；但录音说\n的是找不到合身的，从未提到买不起、太贵，价格问题纯属杜撰。\nB) 未提及\nShe did not like ready-made clothes. 听着合理，但录音只说商店里没有合她身的，从没\n说她不喜欢现成衣服；她做衣服是因买不到，不是因厌恶成衣。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman plan to do in the future?", "options": ["A) Study fashion design at college.", "B) Improve her marketing strategy.", "C) Add designs for women.", "D) Expand her business."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Expand her business.\n【定位】But I will add more clothes for children, both girls and boys, and possibly even for infants.\nAnd I hope to add to my range of designs for men.\n不过我会增加儿童服装，男孩女孩都做，可能还会做婴幼儿的。我也希望扩充我给男士设计\n的产品线。\n【信号】将来意图信号 I will 与 I hope to。答案信号是 I will add more clothes for children 与 I\nhope to add to my range of designs for men 里的 will、hope to——它们标志将来计\n划，后接的增加品类即答案。\n【替换】add more clothes for children ↔ Expand her business.\n具体加品类等于概括的扩展生意\n【排除】C) 偷换概念\n她确说 add to my range of designs for men，Add designs for women. 却把对象由\nmen（男士）偷换成 women（女士），方向都改错了，且她加的远不止男装。\n借⽤原⽂：add to my range of designs for men\nA) 原词陷阱\n前文主持人问 Are you studying fashion design?，Study fashion design at college. 盗\n用了 fashion design；但她回答 I'm majoring in finance，学的是金融，更没把学时装设\n计当未来计划。\n借⽤原⽂：Are you studying fashion design?\nB) 未提及\nImprove her marketing strategy. 听着像生意人会做的事，但录音谈未来时只说加品类、\n保持线上控成本，从没提改进营销策略。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What have researchers done for the first time in history?", "options": ["A) Utilizing artificial intelligence to find a powerful new antibiotic.", "B) Discovering bacteria which are resistant to all known antibiotics.", "C) Identifying bacterial strains that are most harmful to human health.", "D) Removing a deadly strain of bacteria in humans with a new antibiotic."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Utilizing artificial intelligence to find a powerful new antibiotic.\n【定位】The researchers say this is the first time that artificial intelligence has been used to find a\npowerful new antibiotic molecule.\n研究者表示，这是首次有人用人工智能来发现一种强效的新型抗生素分子。\n【信号】first time 与题干同标志。录音里 this is the first time that artificial intelligence has\nbeen used to find a powerful new antibiotic molecule 出现了与题干 for the first time\n完全对应的标志短语 the first time，听到这个标志就锁定这句是答案句。\n【替换】artificial intelligence has been used to find a powerful new antibiotic molecule ↔\nUtilizing artificial intelligence to find a powerful new antibiotic\n被动转主动/同义改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 Discovering bacteria which are resistant to all known antibiotics 盗用了录音第一\n段 resistant to all known antibiotics 这串原词，但录音说的是新抗生素清除了这些耐药\n菌、不是去发现耐药菌，听到熟悉的 resistant 就选会落入陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：resistant to all known antibiotics\nC) 未提及\nC 项 Identifying bacterial strains that are most harmful to human health 说识别最有\n害的菌株，录音通篇没说研究者去给菌株按危害排序或识别哪种最有害，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 Removing a deadly strain of bacteria in humans with a new antibiotic 盗用了第\n一段 removed deadly strains of bacteria 的原词，但录音说清除发生在 in mice（小鼠）\n身上、不是 in humans，且本题问的首次成就是用 AI 找药、不是清除细菌，原词对、主体\n与考点都错。\n借⽤原⽂：removed deadly strains of bacteria in mice", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What makes it important to find new antibiotic drugs?", "options": ["A) Ever-increasing strains of bacteria.", "B) Bacteria's resistance to antibiotics.", "C) The similarity between known drugs.", "D) The growing threat of bacteria to health."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Bacteria's resistance to antibiotics.\n【定位】Why does this matter? The answer is antibiotic resistance. This happens when bacteria\ndevelop the ability to survive the medications designed to kill them.\n这为什么重要？答案是抗生素耐药性。当细菌发展出在那些本该杀死它们的药物下存活的能\n力时，耐药性就出现了。\n【信号】自问自答的因果信号。录音用一个自设问 Why does this matter? 紧跟一句直答 The\nanswer is antibiotic resistance，这种自问自答正是篇章独白明示原因的强信号，答案就藏\n在 The answer is 之后。\n【替换】The answer is antibiotic resistance ↔ Bacteria's resistance to antibiotics\n同义短语换序表述\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 Ever-increasing strains of bacteria 说细菌菌株不断增多，录音只说耐药性问题在加\n重（the problem is growing），从未说菌株数量在增加，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 The similarity between known drugs 盗用了录音 tend to be very similar to drugs\nalready available 里的 similar，但录音说新药彼此相似是研发不足的表现、不是让找新药\n变重要的原因，原词对、角色错。\n借⽤原⽂：tend to be very similar to drugs already available\nD) 偷换概念\nD 项 The growing threat of bacteria to health 盗用了录音 Antibiotic resistance is a\nserious threat to health and the problem is growing 里的 threat 与 growing，但录音\n说的是耐药性对健康是威胁、主语是耐药性，选项却换成 bacteria 对健康的威胁，把本题\n原因从耐药性偷换成笼统的细菌威胁。\n借⽤原⽂：Antibiotic resistance is a serious threat to health and the problem is growing", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the passage say artificial intelligence is able to do in antibiotic research?", "options": ["A) Dispense with experimental testing.", "B) Predict whether compounds are toxic.", "C) Foresee human reaction to antibiotics.", "D) Combat bacteria's resistance to antibiotics."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Predict whether compounds are toxic.\n【定位】Artificial intelligence is also able to predict if compounds are likely to be toxic.\n人工智能还能够预测某些化合物是否可能有毒。\n【信号】able to 直陈功能。录音 Artificial intelligence is also able to predict if compounds are\nlikely to be toxic 里 is also able to 与题干 is able to 直接呼应，是明示 AI 一项能力的功\n能句，听到 able to 即锁定。\n【替换】predict if compounds are likely to be toxic ↔ Predict whether compounds are toxic\nif 换 whether 的近原词改写\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 项 Dispense with experimental testing 盗用了录音 a few interesting candidates that\nrequire experimental testing 里的 experimental testing，但录音说的是 AI 筛出少数候\n选物仍需做实验测试，恰恰没免去测试，选项却说彻底免去，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：a few interesting candidates that require experimental testing\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 Foresee human reaction to antibiotics 盗用了录音 predict 的近义动作，但录音说\nAI 预测的是化合物是否有毒，从没说预测人体对抗生素的反应，把预测对象由化合物毒性偷\n换成人体反应。\n借⽤原⽂：predict if compounds are likely to be toxic\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 Combat bacteria's resistance to antibiotics 盗用了全文反复出现的 antibiotic\nresistance 这一热词，但本题问的是 AI 本身能做什么（筛分子、预测毒性），AI 并不直接\n对抗耐药性、对抗耐药性是研发新药的最终目的，原词对、答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Antibiotic resistance is a serious threat to health", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "According to the passage, how do most people detect lying?", "options": ["A) By theorization.", "B) By generalization.", "C) By observation.", "D) By conversation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By observation.\n【定位】We may develop that theory from observations of those people we know well and see\nregularly, but we tend to generalize what we gather from that unscientific daily research and\nmake it a universal theory.\n我们可能是从对身边熟识、常见之人的观察中形成那套\"看人说谎\"的理论的，但我们往往会\n把从这种不科学的日常观察里得来的东西加以归纳，把它当成放之四海皆准的理论。\n【信号】起源信号 develop … from。develop that theory from observations 里的 develop …\nfrom 点明\"这套判断说谎的理论来自哪里\"，正是\"靠什么方式\"的答案落点；后面虽有\ngeneralize，但那是把观察所得再加工，最初的获取方式仍是 observations。\n【替换】from observations of those people we know well ↔ By observation.\n复数 observations 概括为 observation\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音反复出现 theory、universal theory，A 项 By theorization.（靠理论化）就是拿这个\n原词来骗；但录音说理论是\"从观察中得来\"，获取方式是观察，不是\"靠理论化\"去判断说\n谎。\n借⽤原⽂：make it a universal theory\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说了 we tend to generalize，B 项 By generalization. 就盗这个词；但\ngeneralize 是\"把观察所得再归纳推广\"的后续动作，不是\"多数人最初靠什么发现说谎\"——\n题问的是 detect 的方式，那是观察。\n借⽤原⽂：we tend to generalize what we gather\nD) 未提及\nBy conversation.（靠交谈）与 observation 词形相近，最易听混，但录音通篇没说人们\n靠\"交谈\"来识谎，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the passage say about most lies?", "options": ["A) They are easy to detect.", "B) They are well intended.", "C) They are groundless.", "D) They are harmless."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are harmless.\n【定位】On average, we're lied to some 200 times per day. These are mostly harmless lies, but\nlies nonetheless.\n平均而言，我们每天被骗约 200 次。这些大多是无害的谎言，但仍然是谎言。\n【信号】直接定性 mostly harmless lies。These are mostly harmless lies 一句直接给 most lies\n下定性词 harmless（无害），是答案句；后面 but lies nonetheless 只是强调它们仍算谎\n言，不改\"无害\"这一性质。\n【替换】mostly harmless lies ↔ They are harmless.\n原词 harmless 几乎直给\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are easy to detect. 与录音相反：后文 fails to do better than chance 说人识谎并不\n强、谎言并不\"易察觉\"，此项反了。\n借⽤原⽂：fails to do better than chance\nB) 未提及\nThey are well intended.（出于善意）听上去顺理成章，但录音只说谎言\"无害\"，从没说它\n们\"出于善意\"，是未提及。\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音说人们的识谎\"理论\"有问题（there's a problem with our theories），They are\ngroundless. 像是把\"无根据\"安到了 lies 头上；但题问的是 most lies，谎言本身并未被说\n成\"无根据\"，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：there's a problem with our theories", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What have many studies uncovered about the average person's lie detection?", "options": ["A) Mostly by chance.", "B) Basically objective.", "C) Subject to their mental alertness.", "D) Dependent on their analytical ability."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Mostly by chance.\n【定位】The average person—you and me—tested rigorously on how well we detect lies fails to do\nbetter than chance.\n普通人——你我这样的人——在被严格测试识谎能力时，表现并不比碰运气更好。\n【信号】结论信号 fails to do better than chance。fails to do better than chance 是这段的研\n究结论落点：普通人识谎\"不比瞎猜强\"；下一句 That's well established over many\nstudies 又点明这是多项研究确证的，正对题干 many studies。\n【替换】fails to do better than chance ↔ Mostly by chance.\n\"不比碰运气强\"=\"基本靠运气\"\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nBasically objective.（基本客观）与录音相反：录音说普通人识谎不比碰运气强，谈不上客\n观可靠，方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：fails to do better than chance\nC) 未提及\nSubject to their mental alertness.（取决于心理警觉）听着像研究会得出的结论，但录音\n只说\"不比碰运气强\"，从没说识谎\"取决于警觉性\"，是未提及。\nD) 未提及\nDependent on their analytical ability.（取决于分析能力）同样未被录音提及；录音的结\n论是结果\"靠运气\"，而非把识谎归因于某种能力。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What advice does the new research offer regarding lie detection?", "options": ["A) Looking the speaker in the eye.", "B) Listening carefully to the speaker.", "C) Measuring the speaker's breathing rate.", "D) Focusing on the speaker's facial expressions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Listening carefully to the speaker.\n【定位】The new research offers some surprising advice. Stop looking and listen instead.\n新研究给出了一个出人意料的建议：别再看了，改成去听。\n【信号】建议+对比信号 Stop … and … instead。Stop looking and listen instead 是直白的祈使\n建议句，且用 Stop looking 与 listen instead 构成\"舍看取听\"的对比，落点明确在\"听\"；前\n句 offers some surprising advice 已预告这就是建议。\n【替换】listen instead ↔ Listening carefully to the speaker.\nlisten=Listening to the speaker\nfocus on the voice of the person in question ↔ Listening carefully to the speaker.\n关注声音=认真听说话者\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nLooking the speaker in the eye. 属\"看\"派，而录音建议恰恰是 Stop looking（别看\n了）；它盗用了\"看\"这个动作来骗，方向却与建议相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Stop looking\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音的 breathing 出现在第 3 段——说骗过测谎仪靠 training yourself in breathing\ntechniques，与\"新研究的建议\"无关；Measuring the speaker's breathing rate. 把这个原\n词搬来当建议，属张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：training yourself in breathing techniques\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说 if we're unable to see the face 时准确率反而上升，Focusing on the speaker's\nfacial expressions. 把 face 搬来，方向却相反——建议是别盯脸、去听，看表情正是要放\n弃的做法。\n借⽤原⽂：if we're unable to see the face", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about most American hospitals?", "options": ["A) They don't treat patients with due respect.", "B) They witness a lot of doctor-patient conflicts.", "C) They have to deal with social workers' strikes.", "D) They don't care how much patients have to pay."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They don't treat patients with due respect.\n【定位】What patients want is to be treated with respect and consideration. But in my experience, too\nfew hospitals and doctors are ready to do that.\n病人想要的是被尊重、被体谅地对待。但据我的经验，愿意这样做的医院和医生太少了。\n【信号】转折 But + 程度词 too few。答案落在转折词后：But in my experience, too few\nhospitals and doctors are ready to do that。前半句说『病人想被尊重』是铺垫，But 之\n后才是主讲人的真实判断——能做到的医院太少，这正是定性句。\n【替换】too few hospitals and doctors are ready to do that ↔ They don't treat patients with due respect.\n肯否定转换：很少做到=不做到\ntreated with respect and consideration ↔ with due respect\n同义改写：respect=due respect\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThey witness a lot of doctor-patient conflicts. 借用了全篇的医患话题，听着像在讲医患\n关系，但原文只说医院少有尊重病人，从没说医院『目睹大量医患冲突』，属于顺着话题硬\n编。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey have to deal with social workers' strikes. 盗了后文出现的 social worker 一词，制\n造『听到原词就想选』的诱惑。但原文 The hospital social worker strikes me as an idiot\n里 strikes 是『让我觉得』之意（strike sb as），与『罢工 strike』完全无关，且这是讲个\n人遭遇，不是医院的总体特征。\n借⽤原⽂：The hospital social worker strikes me as an idiot\nD) 未提及\nThey don't care how much patients have to pay. 把话题引向『收费』，但全篇主讲人谈\n的是尊重、知情、被动顺从，从未谈医院在不在乎病人花多少钱，费用维度是凭空添加的。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does Karen Brudney suggest patients do?", "options": ["A) Appear submissive and grateful to doctors and nurses.", "B) Express a strong desire to be consulted or informed.", "C) Refrain from saying anything that sounds negative.", "D) Note down the names of all the doctors and nurses."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Note down the names of all the doctors and nurses.\n【定位】Always bring an advocate, that is, any other person with you to the hospital, and write down\nevery single question and the answer, the name of every doctor and nurse. When people know\nyou have their names, they behave better.\n一定要带一个能为你说话的人，也就是任何别的人，陪你去医院，并且把每一个问题和答\n案、每一位医生和护士的名字都写下来。当别人知道你掌握着他们的名字时，他们就会表现\n得更好。\n【信号】建议句信号 Always / write down。建议落在祈使句信号词上：Always bring an\nadvocate 和 write down ... the name of every doctor and nurse。Always 开头的祈使\n句、write down 都是典型的建议语气，是答案句的标志。\n【替换】write down ... the name of every doctor and nurse ↔\nNote down the names of all the doctors and nurses.\n同义词替换 write down=Note down\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nAppear submissive and grateful to doctors and nurses. 盗用了开篇 Appear to be\nsubmissive, humble, grateful and undemanding 的原词。但那是主讲人反讽地复述『医\n院想要病人怎样』，并非 Brudney 给病人的建议；Brudney 恰恰主张病人要主动（带人、\n记名字），与顺从感恩相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Appear to be submissive, humble, grateful and undemanding\nB) 原词陷阱\nExpress a strong desire to be consulted or informed. 盗用了 be consulted or\ninformed 原词。但 Brudney 说的是：病人若表现出想被咨询或知情，反而会被当成难缠的\n人——这是在描述后果，不是建议病人去这么做。\n借⽤原⽂：if you, as a patient, suggest that you might like to control even part of the situation or\nbe consulted or informed, then you are considered difficult\nC) 原词陷阱\nRefrain from saying anything that sounds negative. 与原文 If you say one negative\nthing, they get defensive 的 negative 一词呼应，听着像建议别说负面话。但原文只是陈\n述『说一句负面话他们就会防备』这一现象，Brudney 并没有把『别说负面话』作为建议给\n出，本题问的建议是记名字。\n借⽤原⽂：If you say one negative thing, they get defensive", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do American doctors expect their patients to be, according to Karen Brudney?", "options": ["A) Cooperative.", "B) Appreciative.", "C) Passive.", "D) Responsive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Passive.\n【定位】They want you to be totally passive.\n他们想要你完全被动顺从。\n【信号】want 表期望 + totally 强调。答案句以 They want you to be totally passive 直白给出期\n望：want 正对题干 expect，totally 加重语气强调这正是医院想要的状态，是明确的答案\n落点。\n【替换】They want you to be totally passive ↔ Passive.\n原词直现：passive=Passive\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nCooperative. 听感上像『听话配合』，与 passive 沾边，易误选。但原文用的是 passive\n（被动顺从），强调的是『别插手、别要求』，而非主动『配合』；cooperative 是录音没\n有的词。\nB) 原词陷阱\nAppreciative.（感激的）与开篇 grateful 含义呼应，易让听过 grateful 的人误选。但本题\n问的是答案句 They want you to be totally passive 给出的特质，是 passive 不是感激；\nappreciative 并非此句所述。\n借⽤原⽂：grateful\nD) 与原文相反\nResponsive.（主动回应的）正是 Passive 的反义。原文明说医院要病人 totally passive\n（完全被动），responsive 取了相反方向；它恰恰是病人想被知情、被咨询那种主动姿\n态，而那会被医院当成难缠。\n借⽤原⽂：They want you to be totally passive", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about a team with vitality?", "options": ["A) Its members work together despite risks of failure.", "B) It prioritizes recruiting young energetic members.", "C) Its members stay in touch even after it breaks up.", "D) It grows more and more mature professionally."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Its members stay in touch even after it breaks up.\n【定位】Vitality comes about when the ties people form with their fellow team members are such that\nthey stay connected even after the team breaks up.\n当人们与团队同伴结成的纽带强到即使团队解散后仍保持联系时，活力（vitality）便由此\n产生。\n【信号】下定义句 \"Vitality comes about when…\"。录音用 Vitality comes about when 给\"活\n力\"正式下定义，紧跟其后的 they stay connected even after the team breaks up 就是这\n一概念的核心内容，定义句即答案句。\n【替换】they stay connected even after the team breaks up ↔\nIts members stay in touch even after it breaks up\nstay connected = stay in touch\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的 risks of failure 借用了第 4 段 safe to take risks with each other 里的 risks 一词，\n但那是讲\"优质关系\"、不是 vitality 的定义，听到 risk 就选会偏题。\n借⽤原⽂：safe to take risks with each other\nB) 未提及\nB 说团队 prioritizes recruiting young energetic members（优先招募有活力的年轻成\n员），录音通篇没说招募，纯属臆造，多半是被 vitality 的\"活力\"联想出来的。\nD) 过度推断\nD 称团队 grows more and more mature professionally，录音定义 vitality 时只字未\n提\"专业上越来越成熟\"，这是顺着\"好团队\"想当然推出来的，原文无据。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What do the researchers find out about members of a team?", "options": ["A) Their differences are likely to impact productivity.", "B) Their similarity is conducive to future collaboration.", "C) Their connections strengthen with the passage of time.", "D) Their mutual understanding stems from a common goal."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Their similarity is conducive to future collaboration.\n【定位】After ties are established, similarity strengthens them. As a result, they regard these\nindividuals with greater trust and mutual understanding, which motivates them to seek further\nopportunities for collaboration.\n在纽带建立之后，相似性会强化这些纽带。结果，人们会以更大的信任和相互理解看待这些\n相似的人，这促使他们去寻求更多合作的机会。\n【信号】因果信号 \"As a result…seek…collaboration\"。录音先说 similarity strengthens\nthem，再用因果词 As a result 引出 motivates them to seek further opportunities for\ncollaboration，\"相似→促成合作\"这条因果链就是研究结论的落点。\n【替换】similarity strengthens them ↔ Their similarity is conducive to\nstrengthens = conducive to\nseek further opportunities for collaboration ↔ future collaboration\nseek further…collaboration = future collaboration\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 称 differences are likely to impact productivity，但录音研究结论强调的是相似\nsimilarity strengthens them，A 取了相反的一极（differences），且 productivity 录音\n根本没提，方向与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：similarity strengthens them\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说 connections strengthen with the passage of time，借了录音 similarity\nstrengthens them 的 strengthen 一词，却把原因偷换成\"随时间推移\"——录音说让纽带\n变牢的是相似，不是时间。\n借⽤原⽂：similarity strengthens them\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 mutual understanding stems from a common goal，mutual understanding 确\n实出自录音 greater trust and mutual understanding，但录音说相互理解来自\"相似\"、不\n是来自\"共同目标\"（common goal 属第 4 段另一因素），强加了错误因果。\n借⽤原⽂：greater trust and mutual understanding", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do we learn about the team the speaker is currently working in?", "options": ["A) It is characterized by diversity.", "B) Its goals are quite inconsistent.", "C) Its members have similar backgrounds.", "D) It is connected by a unique mechanism."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is characterized by diversity.\n【定位】And yet, the four of us have very little in common and are completely\ndifferent demographically.\n然而，我们四个人几乎没有共同点，在人口特征上完全不同。\n【信号】转折 \"And yet\" 后才是真意。前句说当前团队最 successful and cohesive，紧接 And yet\n转折，引出 have very little in common and are completely different demographically\n——转折之后才是关于这支团队的关键定性。\n【替换】have very little in common and are completely different demographically ↔\ncharacterized by diversity\n\"几乎无共同点、完全不同\" = diversity\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 说团队 goals are quite inconsistent，借了录音 the inconsistency 一词，但那个\ninconsistency 指的是\"研究结论与说话者经历不一致\"，不是说这支团队的目标不一致，原\n词被挪错了对象。\n借⽤原⽂：the inconsistency\nC) 与原文相反\nC 称成员 have similar backgrounds，但录音明说当前团队 have very little in\ncommon、完全不同，C 取了相反极性，是被前文研究讲的\"相似\"带偏。\n借⽤原⽂：have very little in common\nD) 未提及\nD 说团队 connected by a unique mechanism，录音从未提任何\"独特机制\"，是凭\"这么成\n功一定有什么特别机制\"想象出来的，原文无据。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do experts recommend concerning being thrifty?", "options": ["A) Putting aside twenty percent of one's earnings.", "B) Spending in anticipation of becoming wealthy.", "C) Living off a small proportion of one's income.", "D) Saving as much as one can possibly manage."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Putting aside twenty percent of one's earnings.\n【定位】\"Even with an astronomical level of income,\" she wrote, \"to properly build wealth, experts\nrecommend saving 20% of your income and living off the remaining 80%.\"\n她写道：\"即便收入高得惊人，要想真正积累财富，专家也建议把收入的 20% 存起来，靠剩\n下的 80% 生活。\"\n【信号】原词 recommend 直接领出建议。答案句直接出现题干同款词 experts recommend，紧\n跟其后的 saving 20% of your income 就是被推荐的做法——\"建议/推荐\"类动词后面那句\n即答案落点。\n【替换】saving 20% of your income ↔ Putting aside twenty percent of one's earnings\n同义改写 save=put aside、income=earnings\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 的 Spending in anticipation of becoming wealthy 几乎照搬原文 spending in\nanticipation of becoming wealthy，但原文是把它当作让人沦为\"工资奴隶\"的反面行为来\n批评，不是专家推荐的做法，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：spending in anticipation of becoming wealthy\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说靠 a small proportion of one's income（一小部分收入）过活，而原文是靠 the\nremaining 80%（大头）生活、只存 20%，C 把\"靠大部分生活\"偷换成\"靠一小部分生活\"，\n与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：living off the remaining 80%\nD) 偷换概念\nD 的 Saving as much as one can possibly manage（尽量多存）听上去合理，但原文给\n的是精确的\"存 20%\"，并非\"能存多少存多少\"，D 用模糊的最大化偷换了专家给出的确切比\n例。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "How does confidence help people to be thrifty?", "options": ["A) It empowers them to cope with irrational emotions.", "B) It will guarantee the profits from their investments.", "C) It will turn them into successful financial planners.", "D) It enables them to focus on long-term investments."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It enables them to focus on long-term investments.\n【定位】It also takes confidence to invest properly. Instead of making investing decisions with your\nemotions, financial planners advise that you should leave your investments alone and focus\non a long-term investment plan.\n正确投资同样需要信心。理财规划师建议，不要凭情绪做投资决策，而应让投资保持不动、\n专注于一个长期投资计划。\n【信号】Instead of 否定情绪、转出正解。答案句用 Instead of making investing decisions with\nyour emotions 先否定\"凭情绪投资\"，转折出真正建议的做法——专注长期投资计\n划；\"Instead of A, do B\"里 B 才是落点。\n【替换】focus on a long-term investment plan ↔ focus on long-term investments\n近乎同义、概括复述\nleave your investments alone ↔ enables them to focus on long-term investments\n详述→概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说信心\"使人应对 irrational emotions\"，盗用了原文的 emotions 一词，但原文是说\"不\n要用情绪做决策\"（避开情绪），并非\"赋予人应对非理性情绪的能力\"，把\"绕开情绪\"偷换\n成\"驾驭情绪\"。\n借⽤原⽂：making investing decisions with your emotions\nB) 过度推断\nB 说信心 guarantee the profits from their investments（保证投资盈利），原文从未承\n诺\"保证收益\"，只说专注长期计划，guarantee 是过度拔高的绝对化表述。\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说信心 turn them into successful financial planners，盗用了原文出现的 financial\nplanners，但原文里 financial planners 是\"给出建议的人\"，不是说有信心就能\"变成\"理财\n规划师，把建议者的身份安到了普通人身上。\n借⽤原⽂：financial planners advise", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "How do millionaires react when they fail in their investment?", "options": ["A) They count on others to take the responsibility.", "B) They change their investment strategy in time.", "C) They think they themselves are to blame.", "D) They persist rather than get discouraged."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They think they themselves are to blame.\n【定位】Millionaires don't count on anyone else to make them rich, and they don't blame anyone else\nif they fall short.\n百万富翁不指望别人让自己变富，达不到目标时也不怪罪任何人。\n【信号】两个 don't…anyone else 否定推责。答案句连用 don't count on anyone else 和 don't\nblame anyone else 两重否定，把责任牢牢落回自己身上——否定\"怪别人\"等于肯定\"怪自\n己\"，这是答案落点。\n【替换】they don't blame anyone else if they fall short ↔ They think they themselves are to blame\n否定他人=肯定自身（肯否转换）\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说他们 count on others to take the responsibility，盗用了原文的 count on anyone\nelse 和 responsibility，但原文明明是\"don't count on anyone else\"——加了否定。A 把\n原文的否定句正过来用，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：don't count on anyone else\nB) 未提及\nB 说他们 change their investment strategy in time（及时改投资策略），原文谈的是\"失\n利时不怪别人/担责\"，从没说他们会\"改策略\"，属凭常识脑补的未提及项。\nD) 答非所问\nD 的 persist rather than get discouraged（坚持不气馁）说的是\"态度上是否泄气\"，原文\n这句讲的是\"责任归谁\"，D 答的是另一个维度，与本题问的\"react when they fail\"对不上。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "Why does the speaker say millionaires are socially indifferent?", "options": ["A) They do not resist novel lifestyles.", "B) They do not try to keep up with others.", "C) They do not care what they have acquired.", "D) They do not pressure themselves to get rich."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They do not try to keep up with others.\n【定位】Essentially, they don't yield to pressure to buy the latest thing or to keep up with others or\nwhat they have acquired.\n本质上，他们不会屈服于\"去买最新潮的东西、或与他人攀比所拥有之物\"的压力。\n【信号】Essentially 引出本质解释。答案句以 Essentially 领出对\"社会冷漠\"的本质性解释，其中\nkeep up with others 直接说明缘由——他们不攀比，这是答案落点。\n【替换】they don't yield to pressure to buy the latest thing or to keep up with others ↔\nThey do not try to keep up with others\n近乎同词、概括复述\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说他们 do not resist novel lifestyles（不抗拒新潮生活），原文提到的是 lifestyle\ncreep（消费随收入膨胀），并说他们 resist lifestyle creep——是\"抗拒\"，A 借 lifestyle\n一词却把\"抗拒\"说成\"不抗拒\"，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：They resist lifestyle creep\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说他们 do not care what they have acquired（不在乎自己拥有什么），盗用了原文\nwhat they have acquired，但原文是说他们\"不为攀比别人所拥有的而买买买\"，并非\"不在\n乎自己的所有物\"，把宾语对象偷换了。\n借⽤原⽂：keep up with others or what they have acquired\nD) 偷换概念\nD 说他们 do not pressure themselves to get rich（不逼自己变富），原文讲的 pressure\n是\"买最新潮的、与人攀比\"的外部压力，不是\"逼自己变富\"的内部压力，D 把压力的对象配\n错了。\n借⽤原⽂：they don't yield to pressure to buy the latest thing", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) aesthetically\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰形容词 pleasing 的副词\n【依据】审美上令人愉悦 = 建筑美观\nfamous for its __ pleasing architecture 说的是巴斯以\"美观的建筑\"闻名，aesthetically\npleasing 是英文里描述\"赏心悦目、审美上令人愉快\"的高频固定搭配，与后面\"healing\nthermal springs\"（治疗效果）并列，分别从美感和功效两方面定调全城特色。\n【竞争词】K) principally 义为\"主要地\"，与 pleasing 不搭\nprincipally pleasing 意为\"主要令人愉悦\"，不符合英语习惯搭配；且 principally 通常修饰动词\n或限定范围（principally known as 等），更适合放在第 28 题修饰 known。\nM) rhetorically 修辞地 ≠ 美感\nrhetorically 指\"在修辞表达上\"，与建筑外观的视觉美感没有关系，rhetorically pleasing\narchitecture 在英语里不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·修饰形容词 pleasing 的副词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) constructed\n动词\n【词性槽】were ___ → 被动语态过去分词\n【依据】建在泉水之上\nupon which 引导的定语从句修饰 the King's Spring，意为\"罗马浴场和神庙建在国王泉之\n上\"，constructed（建造）正是该语义的标准词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·were ___ → 被动语态过去分词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) principally\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰过去分词 known 的副词\n【依据】\"主要被认作罗马 & 乔治亚城\"对应 although 让步\n全句 Although Bath is __(28)__ known as a Roman and Georgian city, many people\ncame in the intervening centuries 用 although 让步：\"尽管巴斯主要以罗马 / 乔治亚之\n城闻名，但中间几个世纪也有很多人来\"。principally\"主要地\"恰好建立\"主流认知 vs. 例外\n史实\"的对照，是该让步逻辑的标准副词。\n【竞争词】M) rhetorically 修辞地 known 不成立\nrhetorically known 在英文中不成习惯搭配，且\"修辞上被认作\"与让步逻辑无关；本句讨论的是\n事实层面的主流印象，与修辞无关。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— rhetorically 词性也对、形态也对，唯独搭配不通。副词槽\nAlthough Bath is __(28)__ known as a Roman and Georgian city 留下 principally 与\nrhetorically 两词同时过关词性，词义又都比较\"专业\"，容易让考生选错。防坑：看\nalthough 让步逻辑——只有\"主要地\"才能与让步对照（虽然主要是 A，但也有 B），\nrhetorically 没有让步含义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·修饰过去分词 known 的副词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) natural\n形容词\n【词性槽】the + 空 + 复数名词 waters → 修饰名词的形容词\n【依据】natural waters = 天然温泉\n上文 healing thermal springs 已点明巴斯的水是\"天然温泉\"，本句指\"中间几个世纪人们来\n利用这些（天然温泉）水\"，natural 直接呼应温泉的天然属性，搭配 natural waters 是英\n语描述天然泉水的常见说法。\n【竞争词】G) legitimate \"合法的水\"语义荒谬\nlegitimate 指\"合法的、婚生的\"，与温泉水的属性毫不沾边；且 legitimate 在后文 unable to\nproduce a __(31)__ son 里有更贴合的位置（legitimate son = 婚生子）。\nO) versatile \"多才多艺的水\"不通\nversatile 多形容人或物用途广，搭配 waters 不通顺；且全文没出现需要 versatile 的语境，它\n是典型的干扰词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the + 空 + 复数名词 water"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) previous\n形容词\n【词性槽】it was ___ generations who paved → 强调结构中修饰复数名词的形容词\n【依据】previous generations 与下文乔治亚时代构成时间对照\n本句让步逻辑是 While the Georgians made 'taking the waters' or bathing particularly\nfashionable, it was __(30)__ generations who paved the way——虽然是乔治亚人让\"浴\n疗\"流行起来，但真正铺路的是\"前几代人\"。previous generations\"前几代人\"正好与 the\nGeorgians（乔治亚一代）形成时间上的对照，且与后文查理二世、詹姆士与玛丽这些\"前任\n王朝\"事例呼应。\n【竞争词】O) versatile \"多才多艺的几代人\"与铺路逻辑无关\nversatile generations 既不构成时间对照，也与\"paved the way（铺路）\"的因果关系无关；\nversatile 整篇都找不到落脚点，是命题人埋的干扰词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·it was ___ generatio"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) legitimate\n形容词\n【词性槽】a + 空 + 名词 son → 修饰 son 的形容词\n【依据】legitimate son = 婚生子（合法继承人）\n查理二世\"desperate for an heir\"——急需王位继承人；legitimate son 在英语里专指\"婚\n生子\"，即可以合法继承王位的儿子。下文 Craving for a male heir 再次印证关注点在合法\n男嗣，与 legitimate 完美吻合。\n【竞争词】O) versatile 多才多艺与王位继承无关\nversatile son\"多才多艺的儿子\"与文意\"急需合法继承人\"完全错位；查理二世在意的不是儿子才\n艺，而是儿子是否婚生、是否能继位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a + 空 + 名词 son → 修饰"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) remedy\n动词\n【词性槽】do something to + 动词原形 → 不定式动词\n【依据】remedy the situation = 补救局面\n查理二世来巴斯泡温泉 in the hope that their magical powers would do something to\n__ the situation，希望温泉魔力\"做点什么来挽救 / 补救（生不出合法继承人的）局面\"。\nremedy 作动词意为\"纠正 / 补救\"，remedy the situation 是英语固定搭配，与 desperate\n的语气一致。\n【竞争词】D) extract \"提取局面\"语义不通\nextract 意为\"提取 / 抽出\"，搭配名词通常是 extract information/data/oil，不能与 situation\n搭配；且与\"求子续嗣\"语境无关，是干扰项。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·do something to + 动词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) offspring\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词性物主代词 their 后 → 名词\n【依据】their offspring = 他们的子嗣\n前文 James and Mary both came to Bath and soon after produced a son 明确他们生\n了一个儿子，本句又紧接\"who was the real father of their __\"，空处应是\"他们的孩子\"。\noffspring 正是\"子女、后代\"的中性书面词，与上下文阴谋论的语气吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词性物主代词 their 后 → 名"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) flocked\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 the whole nation + 空 + to the city → 过去式动词\n【依据】flock to = 蜂拥而至\n前文铺垫\"安妮女王 1702 年访问巴斯，把它坐实为时尚胜地\"once Queen Anne had paid\na visit in 1702, sealing it as the place to be，后面紧接\"全国都___去这座城市\"，逻辑上\n必须是\"大规模、公开地涌向\"，flocked（成群蜂拥而至）正是描写旅游热的标准词，与前\n句\"created something of a boom in tourism\"完美呼应。\n【竞争词】N) sneaked 潜行 ≠ 公开蜂拥\nsneaked 指\"偷偷溜走 / 潜行\"，是小规模、隐秘的动作，主语是\"全国（the whole nation）\"时\n显然不合理；而且与\"boom in tourism\"\"the place to be\"传递的高调、公开氛围正好相反，属\n典型反义诱惑项。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— sneaked 与 flocked 都能搭配 to，词义边界相反。the\nwhole nation __(34)__ to the city 里 sneaked 看似也能填，是典型\"正话反说\"诱惑。防\n坑：看主语量级（the whole nation 是大规模、公开的）+ 上文气氛（boom in\ntourism、the place to be），明显与\"偷偷溜走\"矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 the whole nation"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetically", "B) constructed", "C) designates", "D) extract", "E) flocked", "F) incorporates", "G) legitimate", "H) natural", "I) offspring", "J) previous", "K) principally", "L) remedy", "M) rhetorically", "N) sneaked", "O) versatile"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) incorporates\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 The new Bath Spa + 空 → 第三人称单数动词\n【依据】incorporate A with B = 把 A 与 B 融合\n__(35)__ modern architecture with the ancient spring 的搭配框架\"___ A with B\"暗示\"将\n现代建筑与古泉结合 / 融合\"，incorporates\"把……融合 / 并入\"正是该框架的标准动词，\n与\"new\"（新）和\"ancient\"（古）形成的对比相呼应。\n【竞争词】C) designates designate = \"指定 / 命名\"，搭配 with 不成立\ndesignate 通常搭配 as（designate A as B）或直接接宾语，不与 with 搭配；且语义\"指定 / 命\n名\"与\"把现代建筑与古泉融合\"完全不同，代入读不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 The new Bath Spa"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Some children on the remote islands won't eat their meals because they are fed cheap junk food.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】孩子不肯吃饭+垃圾零食\n【定位】The mothers are concerned they are dealing with indigestion, but it's because they are feeding\nthe children packaged chips which are cheap and convenient.\n母亲们担心孩子是消化不良，但其实是因为她们给孩子吃了既便宜又方便的袋装薯片。\n【改写】children ↔ their children\n同词复现\nwon't eat their meals ↔ refuse to eat their meals\n助动词否定替换实义动词\ncheap junk food ↔ packaged chips which are cheap and convenient\n上位词概括下位实例", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Unlike other parts of Bangladesh, the number of women who die from giving birth remains high on the river islands.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】孕产妇死亡率+岛屿例外\n【定位】There are no doctors within miles, and while child mortality and maternal death have gone\ndown in the rest of the country, this is not the case for the islands.\n周围几英里内都没有医生，而虽然儿童死亡率与孕产妇死亡率在该国其他地方都下降了，岛\n上却并非如此。\n【改写】women who die from giving birth ↔ maternal death\n术语口语化解释\nUnlike other parts of Bangladesh ↔ while ... have gone down in the rest of the country\n让步连词替换\nremains high on the river islands ↔ this is not the case for the islands\n否定下降即维持高位\n【辨邻】 F F 段虽然 Over 70% of TD's 3,000 patients are female 都涉及女性，但话题是女性占\n患者比例与早婚早育，没有讨论 women who die from giving birth 这一死亡率话\n题，因此不是源头段。\nOver 70% of TD's 3,000 patients are female", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "One big problem many islanders have is that they can't afford the prescribed medicines, even with discounts offered.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】买不起处方药+有折扣仍贵\n【定位】The organizations are linked to local pharmacies and offer discounts to the patients and make\nsure to prescribe the most cost-effective brands, but still many residents can't afford even that.\n这些组织已经对接了当地药店、给患者提供折扣、并尽量开最具性价比的品牌，但仍有许多\n居民连这样的价格都负担不起。\n【改写】can't afford the prescribed medicines ↔\nthe medicines they are prescribed are sometimes unaffordable\n形容词转动词短语\neven with discounts offered ↔ offer discounts to the patients\n并列让步压缩成介词短语", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "TD is a virtual medical service financially supported by one of the nation's nonprofit organizations.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】TD 由非营利组织资助\n【定位】It is funded by a nonprofit organization founded by Bangladeshi entrepreneurs, finance and\ntechnology professionals.\n它由一家由孟加拉企业家、金融与科技专业人士创办的非营利组织资助。\n【改写】virtual medical service ↔\nDr. Tina Mustahid ... live-streamed ... for free remote medical consultations\n概括上位词复述服务形态\nfinancially supported by one of the nation's nonprofit organizations ↔\nfunded by a nonprofit organization founded by Bangladeshi entrepreneurs, finance and\ntechnology professionals\nfunded = financially supported；the nation's = Bangladeshi\n【辨邻】 C C 段虽然 Now, a new virtual medical service called Teledaktar (TD) 首次正式介绍\n了 TD 是虚拟医疗服务，但只描述了 TD 每周登岛、设临时医疗点的运作方式，没有说\nTD 由谁资助，因此无法承载题干关于资金来源的具体陈述。\na new virtual medical service called Teledaktar (TD) is trying to make health care more\neasily accessible", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "TD doctors are welcome to the islanders because they treat the sick with respect and patience.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】受欢迎+尊重+耐心\n【定位】The reason, explains one resident, might be the simple gesture of treating the island\ninhabitants with respect. \"Dr. Apa is patient,\" he says, \"At government hospitals, the doctors\ntreat us very badly, but here they listen to us, I can repeat myself many times and no one\ngets annoyed.\"\n一位居民解释说，原因可能就在于他们以尊重的姿态对待岛民这一简单的举动。他说：\nDoctor Apa 很有耐心；在公立医院里医生对我们态度很差，但在这里他们会听我们说话，\n我可以反复说很多遍也没人觉得烦。\n【改写】welcome to the islanders ↔ TD's remote consultations seem to be popular\n受欢迎 = popular\ntreat the sick with respect ↔ treating the island inhabitants with respect\n同结构 + 同词复现\npatience ↔ Dr. Apa is patient\n形容词↔名词", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Women islanders tend to have health problems early partly because they get married and give birth early.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】早婚早育→健康问题提前\n【定位】Early marriage and young motherhood, which are prevalent in these parts of Bangladesh, also\ncontribute to the early onset of health problems.\n在孟加拉这些地区相当普遍的早婚与年轻就当母亲，也促成了健康问题的提前出现。\n【改写】get married and give birth early ↔ Early marriage and young motherhood\n名词短语↔动词短语\nhave health problems early ↔ the early onset of health problems\n名词化结果同义\npartly because ↔ also contribute to\n部分原因的两种表达", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "TD doctors make weekly visits to the remote islands to provide services at a temporary medical center.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】每周登岛+临时医疗点\n【定位】Every week, TD's medical operators travel to the islands by boat, carrying a laptop, a portable\nprinter for prescriptions and tools to run basic medical screenings such as blood pressure,\nblood sugar, body temperature and weight. They choose an area of the island with the best\nInternet reception and set up a makeshift medical center which consists of plastic stools and\nsmall tables borrowed from the locals' homes, a tent in case of rain and a sheet that is strung\nup to give the patients privacy during their session.\n每周 TD 的医务人员都会乘船前往岛屿，随身带着笔记本电脑、用于打印处方的便携打印\n机，以及测血压、血糖、体温与体重等基础筛查的工具。他们会挑岛上信号最好的地方，借\n居民家的塑料凳子与小桌子搭起一个临时医疗点，下雨时再搭起一顶帐篷，并拉一块布单为\n患者提供问诊隐私。\n【改写】weekly visits ↔ Every week, TD's medical operators travel to the islands by boat\n频率短语 ↔ 形容词\na temporary medical center ↔ a makeshift medical center\n同义形容词替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "TD doctors provide the islanders with online diagnoses and treatments for common diseases.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】在线诊治常见病\n【定位】But they can write prescriptions, diagnose common ailments—including digestive issues,\njoint pain, skin diseases, fever and the common cold—and refer patients to doctors at\nlocal hospitals.\n但他们可以开处方、诊断包括消化问题、关节疼痛、皮肤病、发烧、普通感冒在内的常见小\n病，并把患者转诊给当地医院的医生。\n【改写】online diagnoses and treatments ↔ write prescriptions, diagnose common ailments\n开处方+诊断 = 诊治；online 隐含\ncommon diseases ↔\ncommon ailments—including digestive issues, joint pain, skin diseases, fever and the common\ncold\n上位词 + 下位实例列举\n【辨邻】 E E 段虽然 I diagnose them through conversation 也提到诊断，但讲的是一次具体的\n母亲带孩子来看消化问题的案例，并没有列出 TD 能诊治哪些常见病的总体定性，因此\n不是题干 common diseases 这一概括陈述的源头段。\nI diagnose them through conversation\nC C 段 tools to run basic medical screenings such as blood pressure, blood sugar,\nbody temperature and weight 只讲基础筛查项目，并没有给出 TD 诊治哪些\ncommon diseases 的清单，因此不是题干 common diseases 的源头段。\ntools to run basic medical screenings such as blood pressure, blood sugar, body temperature\nand weight", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The residents of the river islands have to keep moving their homes because of floods and land erosions.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】反复迁居+洪水侵蚀\n【定位】They are home to over six million people, who face repeated displacement from flooding and\nerosion—which may be getting worse because of climate change—and a range of health\nrisks, including poor nutrition, malaria and other water-borne diseases.\n这些岛屿是六百多万人的家园，他们因洪水与侵蚀而反复被迫迁居——这种情况可能因气候\n变化而变得更糟——还要面对营养不良、疟疾以及其他水媒疾病等一系列健康风险。\n【改写】keep moving their homes ↔ repeated displacement\n名词化 ↔ 动词化\nfloods and land erosions ↔ flooding and erosion\n同根名词改复数\n【辨邻】 B B 段 Victims of erosion lose their houses, agricultural land and jobs as farmers,\nfishermen and day laborers 讲的是侵蚀让人失去房屋、土地与工作，没有强调反复\n迁居这一动作，因此不是题干 keep moving their homes 的源头段。\nVictims of erosion lose their houses, agricultural land and jobs as farmers, fishermen and\nday laborers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Women islanders usually rely on their husbands to get some medicines for them without diagnoses and prescriptions.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】丈夫代买药+无诊断处方\n【定位】It can get expensive to travel to the doctor, so usually the women living on the islands\ndescribe their illness to their husbands. The husbands then go to the pharmacy, try to describe\nthe issue and return home with some random medicines.\n去看医生开销很大，所以岛上的女性平时只能把自己的病情描述给丈夫；丈夫再到药店去，\n尽量把病情转述一遍，然后带一些随机的药回家。\n【改写】rely on their husbands to get some medicines ↔\nThe husbands then go to the pharmacy, try to describe the issue and return home with some\nrandom medicines\n动作链压缩成一句动宾\nwithout diagnoses and prescriptions ↔ describe their illness to their husbands\n流程中无医生介入即无诊断与处方\n【辨邻】 B B 段 one-third of rural households visit unqualified village doctors 描述的是去找\n未受训的村医，没有出现丈夫代女买药这一具体行为，因此不是题干 Q45 的源头段。\none-third of rural households visit unqualified village doctors\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n用 Fazila 的故事引入：孟加拉北部偏远河岛上的居民因路途遥远、医疗匮乏而长期忍\n病不治；岛屿因泥沙堆积与侵蚀不断形成又消失，岛民频繁因洪水与侵蚀而被迫迁\n居，并面临营养不良、疟疾等多种健康风险。\n信息指纹 Fazila 与河岛+迁居\nB 岛上最大威胁是土地侵蚀，其次是医疗匮乏；该国其他地区儿童与孕产妇死亡率下\n降，岛上却没有；公立医院虽免费但排队长、诊断可疑，三分之一农村家庭只能找未\n受训的村医。\n信息指纹 孕产妇死亡率仍高+村医\nC 岛上有句俚语 rog pushai rakha 形容把看病拖到最后；新推出的远程医疗服务 TD 每\n周派人乘船登岛，带笔记本、便携打印机和简单检测工具，借居民家具搭起临时医\n疗点。\n信息指纹 每周乘船+临时医疗点\nD TD 于 2018 年 10 月成立，在孟加拉乡村与三座岛屿设了 8 个中心，由孟加拉企业\n家、金融与科技专业人士创办的非营利组织资助；首席医生 Tina Mustahid 从首都达\n卡直播看诊，被患者亲切称作 Doctor Apa。\n信息指纹 非营利组织资助+8 个中心\nE\nMustahid 医生通过对话诊断；母亲常担心孩子消化不良不肯吃饭，其实是因为给孩子\n吃了便宜便利的袋装薯片毁了胃口；她让母亲减少不健康零食并强调健康营养教育的\n重要性。\n信息指纹 孩子不吃饭+袋装薯片\nF 孟加拉医疗资源严重短缺：医生与人口比远低于世卫推荐，且多集中在城市；TD 患者\n超七成为女性，原因包括不便与男医生交流、识字与出行能力有限，以及早婚早育导\n致健康问题提前出现。\n信息指纹 早婚早育+医患比\nG 对岛上多数 TD 患者来说 Mustahid 是他们见过的第一位大城市医生；TD 医生不处理\n重症或需面诊的情况，但能在线开处方、诊断常见小病并转诊；还为患者提供健康、\n饮食与生活方式建议。\n信息指纹 开处方+诊断常见病+转诊\nH Fazila 在几次问诊之后才说出自己一直困扰的皮肤病；岛上女性看病开销大，平时只\n能向丈夫描述病情，丈夫去药店凭描述买回一些随机的药，对 Fazila 都没用，直到她\n开始找 Doctor Apa。\n信息指纹 丈夫代述病情+随机买药\nI\n其他非营利组织也开始在岛上提供医疗服务：Friendship 经营浮动船医院，在各岛\n停靠两个月一轮；它还运营卫星诊所，由当地居民担任医生与助手分发健康与卫生\n知识。\n信息指纹 Friendship+浮动船医院\nJ TD 仍面临挑战：许多居民抱怨开出的药即使有折扣也仍然买不起，政府投入不够；患\n者常问为什么诊费免费但药费不免，组织已对接药店并尽量开最具性价比的品牌，但\n仍有人负担不起。\n信息指纹 药价负担不起+折扣无效\nK\n尽管如此 TD 远程问诊仍受欢迎，3000 名患者中至少 200 人回访；一位居民指出原因\n在于被尊重地对待——Doctor Apa 很有耐心，公立医院医生态度恶劣，而这里的医生\n会认真倾听，反复说也不嫌烦。\n信息指纹 受尊重+有耐心+回访", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What fact does the author emphasize concerning selective colleges and universities?", "options": ["A) They have been ignoring the training of graduates for the world of work.", "B) They have been doing well in ensuring their students a successful future.", "C) They have been constantly attacked for being too elite and too expensive.", "D) They have been actively engaged in civic life beyond the school campus."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What fact does the author emphasize 明确询问作者强调的事实，对应原文某一处具体表\n述，不要求跨段推断。\n【拆句】主干是 What fact does the author emphasize，concerning selective colleges and\nuniversities 是介词短语后置修饰 fact，相当于 about。无否定、无比较，平铺直叙。\n【定位】Such charges ignore the fact that these institutions continue to prepare students for success in\ntheir work, for thoughtful engagement in civic life, for lifelong learning, and for\nunderstanding the world and those with whom they live.\n这些指控忽视了一个事实：这些院校仍在持续地为学生做准备——为他们的事业成功、公民\n生活的深入参与、终身学习，以及理解世界与共处之人做准备。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey have been ignoring the training of graduates for the world of work 把“忽视”\n这一动作扣到了院校头上，正好与原文相反——原文是说指控者忽视了事实，并不是院\n校忽视培养。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthese institutions continue to prepare students for success in their work ↔\ndoing well in ensuring their students a successful future\nSuch charges ignore the fact that ↔ They have been doing well\n预判说“院校仍在为学生未来成功做准备”，B 项 doing well in ensuring their students\na successful future 正是这一表述的同义替换，故选 B。\nC) 答非所问\nThey have been constantly attacked for being too elite and too expensive 复述的\n是 Selective colleges and universities in the U.S. are under fire for being too elite\nand too expensive 这一事实——但这是别人对院校的指控，不是作者要强调的 fact；\n题干问 What fact does the author emphasize。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey have been actively engaged in civic life beyond the school campus 把原文中\n“院校培养学生参与公民生活”的对象（学生）偷换成了院校本身参与公民生活，主体被\n替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say in arguing for the model of education in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) It has contributed substantially to the nation's overall development.", "B) It has succeeded in maintaining sustainable financial aid programs.", "C) It has given priority to innovative programs for graduate studies.", "D) It has played a central role in attracting international applicants."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say 询问作者具体表态了什么，对应一句具体陈述，仍属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say，in arguing for the model of education in the U.S. 是\n介词短语作状语，交代 say 发生的语境。结构简单。\n【定位】This model of education has long played a central role in creating opportunity, driving\neconomic growth, and spurring innovation.\n这一教育模式长期以来在创造机会、推动经济增长与激发创新方面扮演着核心角色。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncreating opportunity, driving economic growth, and spurring innovation ↔\ncontributed substantially to the nation's overall development\nhas long played a central role ↔ contributed substantially\n三项并列贡献可统一抽象为 the nation's overall development，A 项正是这一上位概\n括，故选 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nsucceeded in maintaining sustainable financial aid programs 把“该模式的整体贡\n献”偷换成了“维持可持续的资助项目”——原文 their financial aid programs make\nthem more accessible than ever 只是说援助项目使学校更可及，没说“可持续维持”，\n更不是作者为之辩护的核心论据。\nC) 无中生有\ngiven priority to innovative programs for graduate studies 把 spurring innovation\n中的 innovation 拼接成“研究生项目优先”，但原文从未谈及研究生项目优先级，是凭空\n捏造。\nD) 无中生有\nplayed a central role in attracting international applicants 套用了原文 played a\ncentral role 的句式，却把宾语换成了 attracting international applicants（吸引国际\n申请人），但原文从未提国际申请人，是用熟悉句式包装的新内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn about the initial proposals concerning the recent tax reform bill?", "options": ["A) They would have stripped many students of life's chances.", "B) They would have deducted graduate student loan interest.", "C) They would have added to many students' financial burden.", "D) They would have increased the number of tuition waivers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about the initial proposals 问关于某物的具体信息，定位句即答案出\n处，属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What do we learn about the initial proposals，concerning the recent tax reform\nbill 是 the initial proposals 的后置修饰。注意虚拟语气 would have made 暗示这些提议\n未实施，比对选项时四个选项都是 would have，与原文虚拟一致。\n【定位】The initial proposals would have made education unaffordable for many by taxing tuition\nwaivers for graduate students and ending deductions for student loan interest.\n最初的提议本来会通过对研究生学费减免征税、取消学生贷款利息抵扣，使许多人负担不起\n教育。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nwould have stripped many students of life's chances 把“加重经济负担”夸大为“剥夺\n人生机会”——原文 unaffordable for many 只到“许多人负担不起”，未到“剥夺人生机\n会”的程度，且 life's chances 范围过宽。\nB) 与原文相反\nwould have deducted graduate student loan interest 把原文 ending deductions\nfor student loan interest 中的“取消抵扣”反过来说成“抵扣”，方向完全相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwould have made education unaffordable for many ↔\nwould have added to many students' financial burden\ntaxing tuition waivers for graduate students and ending deductions for student loan interest ↔\nfinancial burden\nunaffordable for many 与 added to many students' financial burden 在虚拟语气下\n完全等值，故选 C。\nD) 与原文相反\nwould have increased the number of tuition waivers 与原文 taxing tuition waivers\n相反——原文是对学费减免征税（变相缩减或削弱），选项却说“增加学费减免数量”，\n方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do the data show about elite colleges and universities?", "options": ["A) Their graduates lack the rigor required for doing statistical analysis.", "B) Their students prove to be inadequately prepared for their future careers.", "C) Their focus on research is conducive to developing students' critical thinking.", "D) Their liberal arts education enables graduates to excel in whatever field they are in."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do the data show 询问数据呈现的客观结论，对应原文具体陈述句，属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What do the data show about elite colleges and universities，about 短语限定讨\n论对象。注意题干用的是 data show（data 复数对应原文 The data are clear），属于细\n节呼应。\n【定位】The data are clear: a liberal arts education is great career preparation, both for excellent\nlifetime earnings and for satisfaction with the work. ... In fact, liberal arts graduates do\nextremely well in every imaginable field.\n数据很清楚：博雅教育是出色的职业准备，既能带来优厚的终身收入，也能带来工作满意\n度。……事实上，博雅毕业生在每一个可以想象的领域都表现极佳。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nTheir graduates lack the rigor required for doing statistical analysis 与原文\nrigorous analysis of data and facts（严谨的数据与事实分析能力）正相反——原文说\n博雅教育培养这种严谨，选项却说他们缺乏。\nB) 与原文相反\nTheir students prove to be inadequately prepared for their future careers 与原文\na liberal arts education is great career preparation 正相反——原文说“出色的职业准\n备”，选项说“准备不充分”。\nC) 无中生有\nTheir focus on research is conducive to developing students' critical thinking 借\n用了 scholarly research 和 critical thinking 两个原文词，拼出“院校以研究为重心、研\n究有助于培养批判性思维”——但原文只说博雅教育培养批判性思维，并未说研究是其重\n心，更未谈“研究→批判性思维”的因果。\nD) ✓ 正确\nliberal arts graduates do extremely well in every imaginable field ↔\nTheir liberal arts education enables graduates to excel in whatever field they are in\na liberal arts education is great career preparation ↔\nTheir liberal arts education enables graduates to excel\n预判“在任何领域都表现出色”，D 项 excel in whatever field they are in 是其精准同义\n替换，故选 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is an advantage of providing financial aid for students?", "options": ["A) Every student can choose the institution they wish to attend.", "B) All students can benefit from a diversified student population.", "C) All students will be able to earn a place on university campus.", "D) Less privileged students will be more competitive at elite schools."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is an advantage 问具体的某一项优点，定位到原文表述优点的句子即可，属细\n节题。\n【拆句】主干 What is an advantage of providing financial aid for students，of 短语限定\nadvantage 的所属。注意冠词是 an，意为多个优点中的任一个，选项只要原文支持即可，\n不必是唯一优点。\n【定位】Beyond the virtue of access to those who have earned a place at these schools, the diversity of\neconomic backgrounds enhances the education and experience of all of our students.\n除了让那些凭实力争得席位的人获得入学机会这一价值之外，经济背景的多样性还提升了所\n有学生的教育与体验。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nEvery student can choose the institution they wish to attend 谈“学生可以自由选择\n学校”，原文从未涉及“选校自由”这一层，是凭空捏造。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe diversity of economic backgrounds enhances the education and experience of all of our\nstudents\n↔ All students can benefit from a diversified student population\nthe diversity of economic backgrounds ↔ a diversified student population\n原文明确说 all of our students 都从多样性中获益，与 B 项 All students can benefit\n完全对位，故选 B。\nC) 偷换概念\nAll students will be able to earn a place on university campus 把原文 those who\nhave earned a spot（那些已凭实力争得席位的人）扩大为“全体学生都能争得席位”\n——原文说的是 access 给了已经“earned”的人，并不是让所有人都能 earn。\nD) 无中生有\nLess privileged students will be more competitive at elite schools 谈“弱势学生在精\n英校竞争力更强”，原文只说资助让他们有 access、让全体学生从多样性中获益，并未对\n比“是否更具竞争力”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author intend to show by citing the findings from the report published last year?", "options": ["A) People seldom appeal to rationality in their thinking.", "B) It is often the case that truth lies in the hands of a few.", "C) Common sense and science are the two sides of a coin.", "D) Few people know if climate change is really happening."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the author intend to show by citing the findings from the report 同时出\n现 \"intend to show\" 与 \"citing\"，典型的\"作者引用例证想说明什么\"句式。\n【拆句】Q51 主干是 \"What does the author intend to show\"，by 引导方式状语 \"by citing the\nfindings from the report published last year\"，published last year 是过去分词作\nreport 的后置定语；结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】But what do we mean by an appeal to common sense? Presumably it's an appeal to rationality\nof some sort that forms the basis of more complex reasoning. The appeal to common sense,\nhowever, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking that just feels right.\n但我们说\"诉诸常识\"究竟意味着什么？大概是诉诸某种作为更复杂推理基础的理性吧。然\n而，所谓\"诉诸常识\"，往往不过是诉诸\"感觉对\"的想法罢了。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nabout 36% said it was \"common sense\" ... only 11% saying their belief that climate change was\nnot happening was based on scientific research\n↔ People seldom appeal to rationality in their thinking\nThe appeal to common sense, however, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking that\njust feels right\n↔ appeal to rationality in their thinking\n作者列调查只是\"由头\"，紧跟的转折句把\"common sense\"等同于\"feels right\"而非\nrationality；合起来等于说\"人们很少真的诉诸理性\"，与 People seldom appeal to\nrationality in their thinking 直接对接。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈\"大多数人凭感觉判断\"，并未提及 truth lies in the hands of a few 这种\"真理掌\n握在少数人手中\"的命题。\nC) 与原文相反\n作者把\"常识\"与\"科学\"对立，并贬低前者；选项却说二者是 two sides of a coin\"一体两\n面\"，与原文的对立关系正相反。\nD) 答非所问\nFew people know if climate change is really happening 谈的是\"人们是否知道气候\n变化\"，而题干问的是作者引用调查的目的——目的在论证\"常识非理性\"，不在论证\"人\n们对气候变化的认知\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the appeal to common sense according to the author?", "options": ["A) It is the basis for the internalisation of individuals' ideologies.", "B) It is a series of conceptions formulated from complex reasoning.", "C) It is collective wisdom that helps people interact with new ideas.", "D) It is something subjective based on what one perceives to be right."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the appeal to common sense according to the author 直接问一个概念\"是什\n么\"，定义在文中可被直接定位回答。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 \"What is the appeal to common sense\"，according to the author 是介词短\n语作整句的状语，限定信息来源；句式简单。\n【定位】The appeal to common sense, however, is usually nothing more than an appeal to thinking\nthat just feels right, but what feels right to one person may not feel right to another. Whether\nit feels right is usually a reflection of the world view and ideologies we have internalised.\n然而所谓\"诉诸常识\"，往往不过是诉诸\"感觉对\"的想法罢了，可一个人觉得对，另一个人未\n必觉得对。是否觉得对，通常只是我们已经内化的世界观和意识形态的投射。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文是\"内化的意识形态\"决定\"是否感觉对\"——意识形态是因、感觉是果；选项却把\n诉诸常识当成 basis for the internalisation of individuals' ideologies，把因果颠倒\n过来。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 Presumably it's an appeal to rationality of some sort that forms the basis of\nmore complex reasoning 是作者先假设的\"理想状态\"，紧随 however, is usually\nnothing more than 被否定；formulated from complex reasoning 恰是被否定的那\n一面。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文从未说 common sense 是 collective wisdom\"集体智慧\"；作者后文反而把集体智\n慧的代表给了\"science\"而非 common sense，是典型张冠李戴。\nD) ✓ 正确\nan appeal to thinking that just feels right ↔\nsomething subjective based on what one perceives to be right\nwhat feels right to one person may not feel right to another ↔ something subjective\n预判\"凭感觉对的主观判断\"与 something subjective based on what one perceives\nto be right 在两个语义点上完全对齐：subjective ↔ feels right 因人而异；perceives\nto be right ↔ feels right。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does Daniel Kahneman think is the problem of testing new ideas with existing beliefs?", "options": ["A) It may lead to incorrect judgment.", "B) It makes no use of common sense.", "C) It fails to correct mistakes through serious reasoning.", "D) It can produce psychologically unacceptable outcomes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does Daniel Kahneman think is the problem 直接问某人对某事的看法，文中可定\n位到原话或转述。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 \"What does Daniel Kahneman think\"；think 后接省略 that 的宾语从句 \"is\nthe problem of testing new ideas with existing beliefs\"，其中 of 短语作 the problem\n的后置定语；testing... 为动名词作 of 的宾语。\n【定位】As Nobelist Daniel Kahneman notes in Thinking, Fast and Slow, when we arrive at\nconclusions in this way, the outcomes also feel true, regardless of whether they are. We are\nnot psychologically well equipped to judge our own thinking.\n正如诺贝尔奖得主丹尼尔·卡尼曼在《思考，快与慢》中所言，当我们以这种方式得出结论\n时，结果\"感觉上是真的\"，至于它们到底是不是真的并不重要。我们在心理上并不擅长审视\n自己的思考过程。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe outcomes also feel true, regardless of whether they are ↔\nIt may lead to incorrect judgment\nit is more about judging than thinking ↔ incorrect judgment\n两条语义在 P3 里共同作用：判断而非思考、结果感觉真但不一定真——合起来就是\"该\n过程会带来错误判断\"，对应 It may lead to incorrect judgment。\nB) 答非所问\nKahneman 谈的是该过程的\"问题\"，不是评论它\"用没用 common sense\"；makes no\nuse of common sense 把话题从\"问题是什么\"偷换为\"是否使用了常识\"。\nC) 过度推断\n原文只说该过程产生的\"感觉真\"未必真，并未谈到能不能\"通过严肃推理修正错误\"——\n这是基于原文延展出去的额外命题。\nD) 偷换概念\npsychologically unacceptable outcomes 把原文 not psychologically well\nequipped（人在心理上没有能力）偷换成\"心理上无法接受的结果\"——一个谈\"能力\"，\n一个谈\"接受度\"，貌似都有 psychologically 实则不同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What can we do to be less susceptible to cognitive biases?", "options": ["A) Give equal weight to evidence of both sides in a conflict.", "B) Provide convincing examples in developing an argument.", "C) Establish socially shared cognition via scientific methods.", "D) Avoid inconsistencies when addressing controversial issues."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What can we do to be less susceptible to cognitive biases 问做法，原文有明确对策句\n可对应。\n【拆句】Q54 主干 \"What can we do\"；to be less susceptible to cognitive biases 是不定式作目\n的状语；be susceptible to 为固定搭配，意为\"易受……影响\"，less 修饰 susceptible，整\n体表示\"少受影响\"。\n【定位】One way we can check our internal biases and inconsistencies is through the social\nverification of knowledge, in which we test our ideas in a rigorous and systematic way to see\nif they make sense not just to us, but to other people. The outstanding example of this socially\nshared cognition is science.\n我们能够检验自身内在偏差与矛盾的一种方式，是通过对知识的社会化检验：以严格、系统\n的方式检验我们的想法，看它们不仅对自己说得通，对别人也是否说得通。这种社会共享认\n知最杰出的例子就是科学。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nP4 只提到我们对证据有偏向，Give equal weight to evidence of both sides in a\nconflict 把它包装成\"双方等量赋权\"作为对策；原文从未给出这一对策。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈\"用科学的社会共享认知去检验自己的想法\"，并未提及在论证中 Provide\nconvincing examples in developing an argument\"举有说服力的例子\"这一具体写作\n技巧。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthrough the social verification of knowledge ↔ Establish socially shared cognition\nwe test our ideas in a rigorous and systematic way ↔ via scientific methods\n预判\"通过社会化检验、典型即科学\"与 Establish socially shared cognition via\nscientific methods 在两个核心点上同义对齐——前半部分是手段，后半部分是手段的\n载体。\nD) 偷换概念\nAvoid inconsistencies when addressing controversial issues 把 internal biases and\ninconsistencies里的 inconsistencies 搬走，却换上一个原文没谈的 controversial\nissues 场景；并且对策从\"通过社会化检验来检查\"偷换成\"直接避免不一致\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What message does the author try to convey at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Multiple perspectives stimulate people's interest in exploring the unknown.", "B) Individuals can enhance their overall capabilities by interacting with others.", "C) Individuals should think freely to break from the restrictions of common sense.", "D) Collaborative efforts can overcome individuals limitations in scientific inquiry."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】What message does the author try to convey at the end of the passage 同时含\"末\n段\"位置与\"传达的信息\"，是局部主旨题。\n【拆句】Q55 主干 \"What message does the author try to convey\"；at the end of the passage\n是介词短语作地点状语，限定信息发生位置；try to convey 是\"想要传达\"，注意 try to 自\n带\"主观意图\"色彩，对应主旨题问法。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n末段从未谈及\"多元视角能激发人们对未知的兴趣\"——stimulate people's interest in\nexploring the unknown 是被自造出的概念，与原文金句无关。\nB) 以偏概全\nIndividuals can enhance their overall capabilities by interacting with others 把范\n围窄化为\"提升个人综合能力\"，遗漏了原文的\"科学探究\"语境与\"克服局限\"这一关键含\n义；末段强调的是\"集体优于个人\"，不是\"通过互动让个人变强\"。\nC) 与原文相反\n末段批评的正是\"以为自由地思考、不被群体束缚\"的态度（imagine themselves to be\nmore free in their thinking, unbound by involvement with the group）；\nIndividuals should think freely to break from the restrictions of common sense 反\n而把\"自由思考\"当作积极建议——立场正好相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nWe are smarter together than we are individually ↔\nCollaborative efforts can overcome individuals limitations\nmethodological, collaborative investigation ↔ Collaborative efforts ... in scientific inquiry\nin reality they are tightly bound by their capabilities and perspectives ↔\nindividuals limitations\n末段三层语义（个人受限—协作探究—集体更聪明）全部对应到 Collaborative efforts\ncan overcome individuals limitations in scientific inquiry 中，是覆盖最完整的\n选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
